{"level":30,"time":1778246253273,"pid":122,"hostname":"bd047d16-36d","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":9,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JSpVYsLEBDvkebRXcBuN64","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 3 — Block 13\n     2→\n     3→## text_block: List of 16 notes detailing restrictions and requirements for work near electric transmission lines and easements, including clearance zones, safety meetings, access, barricades, and prohibited structures.\n     4→\n     5→Electric Transmission Notes\n     6→1. No trees shall be planted within the transmission easement. No trees shall be planted within 25 feet of the base of the transmission structure. Vehicular access for Austin Energy trucks and equipment is always to be maintained within the easement. Austin Energy will not be responsible for damage and/or removal of vegetation within the easement.\n     7→2. Do not dig or grade within 25 feet of the transmission structures, including down guy anchors. Grading near electric transmission facilities must be coordinated with Austin Energy prior to commencement of grading. Call Andrew Perez at 512-505-7153 to schedule a meeting.\n     8→3. A pre-construction safety meeting is required with Austin Energy 48 hours before commencement of construction. Call Andrew Perez at 512-505-7153 to schedule a tailgate safety meeting. Include Chuck Hendry (ph 505-7151) in the meeting, if cranes are being used during construction. OSHA requires a 20' clearance from energized transmission lines during construction.\n     9→4. Barricades must be erected 10 feet from electric transmission structures during construction.\n    10→5. Any relocation of electric transmission facilities or outages caused by this project will be charged to the property owner and contractor.\n    11→6. Warning signs must be placed under the overhead electric transmission facilities as notification of the electrical hazard.\n    12→7. For safety reasons, aerial equipment, dumpsters, staging or spoils areas are not permitted within 20 feet of the transmission wire and/or structures and must be located outside the easement.\n    13→8. 24-hour access to electric facilities shall be maintained.\n    14→9. Any temporary or permanent fence preventing access to the transmission facilities and/or easement shall be coordinated with Austin Energy staff. AE staff will install a lock on the gate for access.\n    15→10. Property owner and contractor are responsible for dust controls to minimize contamination of wire and insulators caused by dust from this project. Any subsequent cleaning or electrical outages caused by dust from this project will be charged to the property owner and contractor.\n    16→11. Property owner is responsible for any damages to curbing, landscaping, walls, paving placed around the electric transmission structures/poles/lines caused by Austin Energy during maintenance and repairs. All curbing within the electric transmission easement must be laydown curbing.\n    17→12. Roads/driveways/pavement/parking within the easement should be built to support a 46,000 lb tandem axle (5 foot spacing) to ensure safety.\n    18→13. Fire hydrants must be located out of the transmission easement and a minimum of 20 feet from any transmission structure.\n    19→14. Sprinklers are prohibited within 25 feet of transmission poles and structures\n    20→15. Do not dig or grade within 25 feet of the transmission structures, including down guy anchors)\n    21→16. Owner may not place, erect, construct or maintain the following within electric transmission easement:\n    22→  · any permanent structures, including, but not limited to habitable structures such as homes, mobile homes, garages, or offices,\n    23→  · any structure of any kind in such proximity to the electric transmission or distribution lines, poles, structures, towers, or appurtenant facilities as would constitute a violation of the National Electric Safety Code in effect at the time the structure is erected, nor\n    24→  · any structures, including but not limited to, fences, storage sheds, drainage, filtration or detention ponds which would impair Austin Energy's access to the transmission easements or its lines, poles, structures, towers or appurtenant facilities in the easements.\n    25→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2e942253-7258-4c80-b1f5-d3905a77aa10","uuid":"f0c7a13d-8283-4e0c-8c91-0d0d13b6a79d","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/vercel/sandbox/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-03/block-13.md","content":"# Sheet 3 — Block 13\n\n## text_block: List of 16 notes detailing restrictions and requirements for work near electric transmission lines and easements, including clearance zones, safety meetings, access, barricades, and prohibited structures.\n\nElectric Transmission Notes\n1. No trees shall be planted within the transmission easement. No trees shall be planted within 25 feet of the base of the transmission structure. Vehicular access for Austin Energy trucks and equipment is always to be maintained within the easement. Austin Energy will not be responsible for damage and/or removal of vegetation within the easement.\n2. Do not dig or grade within 25 feet of the transmission structures, including down guy anchors. Grading near electric transmission facilities must be coordinated with Austin Energy prior to commencement of grading. Call Andrew Perez at 512-505-7153 to schedule a meeting.\n3. A pre-construction safety meeting is required with Austin Energy 48 hours before commencement of construction. Call Andrew Perez at 512-505-7153 to schedule a tailgate safety meeting. Include Chuck Hendry (ph 505-7151) in the meeting, if cranes are being used during construction. OSHA requires a 20' clearance from energized transmission lines during construction.\n4. Barricades must be erected 10 feet from electric transmission structures during construction.\n5. Any relocation of electric transmission facilities or outages caused by this project will be charged to the property owner and contractor.\n6. Warning signs must be placed under the overhead electric transmission facilities as notification of the electrical hazard.\n7. For safety reasons, aerial equipment, dumpsters, staging or spoils areas are not permitted within 20 feet of the transmission wire and/or structures and must be located outside the easement.\n8. 24-hour access to electric facilities shall be maintained.\n9. Any temporary or permanent fence preventing access to the transmission facilities and/or easement shall be coordinated with Austin Energy staff. AE staff will install a lock on the gate for access.\n10. Property owner and contractor are responsible for dust controls to minimize contamination of wire and insulators caused by dust from this project. Any subsequent cleaning or electrical outages caused by dust from this project will be charged to the property owner and contractor.\n11. Property owner is responsible for any damages to curbing, landscaping, walls, paving placed around the electric transmission structures/poles/lines caused by Austin Energy during maintenance and repairs. All curbing within the electric transmission easement must be laydown curbing.\n12. Roads/driveways/pavement/parking within the easement should be built to support a 46,000 lb tandem axle (5 foot spacing) to ensure safety.\n13. Fire hydrants must be located out of the transmission easement and a minimum of 20 feet from any transmission structure.\n14. Sprinklers are prohibited within 25 feet of transmission poles and structures\n15. Do not dig or grade within 25 feet of the transmission structures, including down guy anchors)\n16. Owner may not place, erect, construct or maintain the following within electric transmission easement:\n  · any permanent structures, including, but not limited to habitable structures such as homes, mobile homes, garages, or offices,\n  · any structure of any kind in such proximity to the electric transmission or distribution lines, poles, structures, towers, or appurtenant facilities as would constitute a violation of the National Electric Safety Code in effect at the time the structure is erected, nor\n  · any structures, including but not limited to, fences, storage sheds, drainage, filtration or detention ponds which would impair Austin Energy's access to the transmission easements or its lines, poles, structures, towers or appurtenant facilities in the easements.\n","numLines":25,"startLine":1,"totalLines":25}}}
{"level":30,"time":1778246253274,"pid":122,"hostname":"bd047d16-36d","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":9,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QzwUcxkPPWYYW5V83tbE2h","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 3 — Block 17\n     2→\n     3→## text_block: Additional notes outlining requirements from Austin Energy regarding easements, tree work, erosion control, and safety clearances. Contains information regarding the developer's responsibility for tree pruning, erosion control, and maintaining clearances according to safety codes.\n     4→\n     5→Austin Energy has the right to prune and/or remove trees, shrubbery and other obstructions to the extent necessary to keep the easements Austin Energy will perform all tree work in compliance with Chapter 25-8, Subchapter B of the City of Austin Land Development Code.\n     6→\n     7→The owner/developer of this subdivision/lot shall provide Austin Energy with any easement and/or access required, in addition to those indicated, for the installation and ongoing maintenance of overhead and underground electric facilities. These easements and/or access are required to provide electric service to the building and will not be located so as to cause the site to be out of compliance with Chapter 25-8 of the City of Austin Land Development Code.\n     8→\n     9→The owner shall be responsible for installation of temporary erosion control, revegetation and tree protection. In addition, the owner shall be responsible for any initial tree pruning and tree removal that is within ten feet of the center line of the proposed overhead electrical facilities designed to provide electric service to this project. The owner shall include Austin Energy's work within the limits of construction for this project.\n    10→\n    11→The owner of the property is responsible for maintaining clearances required by the National Electric Safety Code, Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA) regulations, City of Austin rules and regulations and Texas state laws pertaining to clearances when working in close proximity to overhead power lines and equipment. Austin Energy will not render electric service unless required clearances are maintained. All costs incurred because of failure to comply with the required clearances will be charged to the owner.\n    12→\n    13→Any relocation of electric facilities shall be at landowner's/developer’s expense.\n    14→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2e942253-7258-4c80-b1f5-d3905a77aa10","uuid":"8c9df738-4879-451c-896e-20f4eb76c385","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/vercel/sandbox/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-03/block-17.md","content":"# Sheet 3 — Block 17\n\n## text_block: Additional notes outlining requirements from Austin Energy regarding easements, tree work, erosion control, and safety clearances. Contains information regarding the developer's responsibility for tree pruning, erosion control, and maintaining clearances according to safety codes.\n\nAustin Energy has the right to prune and/or remove trees, shrubbery and other obstructions to the extent necessary to keep the easements Austin Energy will perform all tree work in compliance with Chapter 25-8, Subchapter B of the City of Austin Land Development Code.\n\nThe owner/developer of this subdivision/lot shall provide Austin Energy with any easement and/or access required, in addition to those indicated, for the installation and ongoing maintenance of overhead and underground electric facilities. These easements and/or access are required to provide electric service to the building and will not be located so as to cause the site to be out of compliance with Chapter 25-8 of the City of Austin Land Development Code.\n\nThe owner shall be responsible for installation of temporary erosion control, revegetation and tree protection. In addition, the owner shall be responsible for any initial tree pruning and tree removal that is within ten feet of the center line of the proposed overhead electrical facilities designed to provide electric service to this project. The owner shall include Austin Energy's work within the limits of construction for this project.\n\nThe owner of the property is responsible for maintaining clearances required by the National Electric Safety Code, Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA) regulations, City of Austin rules and regulations and Texas state laws pertaining to clearances when working in close proximity to overhead power lines and equipment. Austin Energy will not render electric service unless required clearances are maintained. All costs incurred because of failure to comply with the required clearances will be charged to the owner.\n\nAny relocation of electric facilities shall be at landowner's/developer’s expense.\n","numLines":14,"startLine":1,"totalLines":14}}}
{"level":30,"time":1778246253276,"pid":122,"hostname":"bd047d16-36d","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":9,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DtxLdaPehjnkRTHn79WoVT","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 3 — Block 19\n     2→\n     3→## text_block: List of 5 notes regarding electric utilities, specifically referencing Austin Energy's requirements for tree pruning, easements, clearances, and relocation costs.\n     4→\n     5→Electric Notes\n     6→1. Austin Energy has the right to prune and/or remove trees, shrubbery and other obstructions to the extent necessary to keep the easements clear. Austin Energy will perform all tree work in compliance with Chapter 25-8, Subchapter B of the City of Austin Land Development Code.\n     7→2. The owner/developer of this subdivision/lot shall provide Austin Energy with any easement and/or access required, in addition to those indicated, for the installation and ongoing maintenance of overhead and underground electric facilities. These easements and/or access are required to provide electric service to the building and will not be located so as to cause the site to be out of compliance with Chapter 25-8 of the City of Austin Land Development Code.\n     8→3. The owner shall be responsible for installation of temporary erosion control, revegetation and tree protection. In addition, the owner shall be responsible for any initial tree pruning and tree removal that is within ten feet of the center line of the proposed overhead electric facilities designed to provide electric service to this project. The owner shall include Austin Energy's work within the limits of construction for this project.\n     9→4. The owner of the property is responsible for maintaining clearances required by the National Electric Safety Code, Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA) regulations, City of Austin rules and regulations and Texas state laws pertaining to clearances when working in close proximity to overhead power lines and equipment. Austin Energy will not render electric service unless require clearances are maintained. All costs incurred because of failure to comply with the required clearances will be charged to the owner.\n    10→5. Any relocation of electric facilities shall be at the landowner's/developer's expense.\n    11→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2e942253-7258-4c80-b1f5-d3905a77aa10","uuid":"5f19bdac-a0f1-49b4-ab4d-f741a3ac3594","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/vercel/sandbox/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-03/block-19.md","content":"# Sheet 3 — Block 19\n\n## text_block: List of 5 notes regarding electric utilities, specifically referencing Austin Energy's requirements for tree pruning, easements, clearances, and relocation costs.\n\nElectric Notes\n1. Austin Energy has the right to prune and/or remove trees, shrubbery and other obstructions to the extent necessary to keep the easements clear. Austin Energy will perform all tree work in compliance with Chapter 25-8, Subchapter B of the City of Austin Land Development Code.\n2. The owner/developer of this subdivision/lot shall provide Austin Energy with any easement and/or access required, in addition to those indicated, for the installation and ongoing maintenance of overhead and underground electric facilities. These easements and/or access are required to provide electric service to the building and will not be located so as to cause the site to be out of compliance with Chapter 25-8 of the City of Austin Land Development Code.\n3. The owner shall be responsible for installation of temporary erosion control, revegetation and tree protection. In addition, the owner shall be responsible for any initial tree pruning and tree removal that is within ten feet of the center line of the proposed overhead electric facilities designed to provide electric service to this project. The owner shall include Austin Energy's work within the limits of construction for this project.\n4. The owner of the property is responsible for maintaining clearances required by the National Electric Safety Code, Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA) regulations, City of Austin rules and regulations and Texas state laws pertaining to clearances when working in close proximity to overhead power lines and equipment. Austin Energy will not render electric service unless require clearances are maintained. All costs incurred because of failure to comply with the required clearances will be charged to the owner.\n5. Any relocation of electric facilities shall be at the landowner's/developer's expense.\n","numLines":11,"startLine":1,"totalLines":11}}}
{"level":30,"time":1778246253534,"pid":122,"hostname":"bd047d16-36d","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":3,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen_01KR3VRQP882VAQ550C43MPA4V","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JxzV6mposM8AjJMTG8G8bj","name":"mcp__conductor_tools__vision","input":{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNum":31,"prompt":"Identify all overhead electric (OHE) lines shown on this landscape plan. For each overhead electric line or facility, please describe:\n1. The location on the site (which portion of the property)\n2. The routing direction/orientation (north-south, east-west, diagonal)\n3. The proximity to any proposed buildings, parking areas, trees, or other site features\n4. Whether the lines appear to cross over buildings or site features\n5. Any visible clearance dimensions or annotations related to overhead electric lines\n6. The condition of overhead poles or structures (if visible)\n\nAlso describe the overall site layout showing where the 8 buildings are located and where the detention pond is located to help establish the spatial context for the overhead lines."}}],"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7700aa25-0227-4e8e-93af-3aee77428b5b","uuid":"69763234-9686-4bff-ae3b-13e4aa4ee6f2"}
{"level":30,"time":1778246253567,"pid":122,"hostname":"bd047d16-36d","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":3,"runIndex":"run-1","documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNum":31,"prompt":"Identify all overhead electric (OHE) lines shown on this landscape plan. For each overhead electric line or facility, please describe:\n1. The location on the site (which portion of the property)\n2. The routing direction/orientation (north-south, east-west, diagonal)\n3. The proximity to any proposed buildings, parking areas, trees, or other site features\n4. Whether the lines appear to cross over buildings or site features\n5. Any visible clearance dimensions or annotations related to overhead electric lines\n6. The condition of overhead poles or structures (if visible)\n\nAlso describe the overall site layout showing where the 8 buildings are located and where the detention pond is located to help establish the spatial context for the overhead lines.","msg":"Calling vision tool: 1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777 (31)"}
{"level":30,"time":1778246256643,"pid":122,"hostname":"bd047d16-36d","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":7,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen_01KR3VRXH9HDD227KXPR4RDKBW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Perfect! Block 2 and Block 5 confirm:\n- Block 2 states: \"Utility lines such as water (W), storm drain (SD), and overhead electric (OHE)\" are shown on the landscape plan\n- Block 5 defines the legend including: \"SY - STREET YARD TREE (EXCESS OF 1.5 CAL. INCH COUNTING TOWARDS MITIGATION), PL - PARKING LOT TREE, P - PARKING BUFFER POINT TREE, M - MITIGATION TREE\"\n\nSo the project DOES have overhead electric lines (OHE) shown on the Landscape Plan, and there ARE trees proposed on the landscape plan. This means the grouping EL-2 (Tree Clearances from Overhead Electric Lines) IS APPLICABLE.\n\nNow I need to:\n1. Get more details about the actual trees shown and their locations relative to the overhead electric lines\n2. Check the plant schedule to verify species and mature heights\n3. Determine if any trees are within the required clearance zones\n4. Check if overhead electric facilities are shown on other sheets (Site Plan, Utility Plan, Grading Plan)\n\nLet me check if there's a plant schedule or tree list. According to the README, Sheet 32 is the \"Tree List\" and Sheet 35 is \"Landscape Calculations\" with plant lists. Let me also check if there are any visual details I can extract from the landscape plan itself using vision.\n\nFirst, let me read the Tree List (Sheet 32) guide.","signature":""}],"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4b3284e2-1747-4d44-a558-2f00d9a24819","uuid":"ece505ee-c95d-4019-97e8-d1e674c06096"}
