{"level":30,"time":1778246271665,"pid":122,"hostname":"bd047d16-36d","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNum":31,"prompt":"Examine the landscape plan for this project. Please identify:\n1. All overhead electric (OHE) utility lines shown on the plan\n2. The locations and approximate distances of all proposed trees (both shade trees and ornamental trees) relative to the overhead electric lines\n3. Any areas where trees are shown in proximity to overhead electric facilities\n4. Whether the overhead electric lines are distribution or transmission based on their characteristics (pole size, conductor spacing, etc.)\nProvide specific locations using site features (buildings, parking areas, etc.) as reference points.","res":{"steps":[{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on a visual analysis of the provided landscape plan (Sheet 28 of 35, \"LANDSCAPE PLAN\" for \"VALLEY VIEW TOWNHOMES\"), here are the findings regarding the overhead electric lines and proposed trees:\n\n**1. Identification of Overhead Electric (OHE) Utility Lines**\nThere is one overhead electric utility line clearly identified on the plan. It runs horizontally along the entire bottom (southern) boundary of the site plan area shown. The line is denoted by a solid line interrupted periodically by the text label \"**-OHE-**\". There are also small symbols (a cross inside a circle) placed intermittently along this line, which typically denote utility poles.\n\n**2. Locations and Approximate Distances of Proposed Trees Relative to OHE Lines**\nSeveral trees are proposed along the southern edge of the property, running parallel to the identified -OHE- line. Using the graphic scale provided in the bottom right corner (Scale in Feet, 1\" = 20'-0\"), here are the approximate locations and distances of the tree trunks (center points of the tree symbols) relative to the -OHE- line, reading from left to right along the bottom edge:\n\n*   **Bottom Left Area (Southwest corner of buildings):** There is one large tree symbol proposed here. The center of this tree appears to be approximately **10 to 15 feet** north of the -OHE- line. The drawn mature canopy radius extends to or slightly overlaps the -OHE- line.\n*   **Bottom Middle Area (South of the main building block):** There is a cluster of three smaller tree symbols (likely ornamental/understory) located between the building/sidewalk and the property line. The centers of these trees appear to be roughly **10 to 15 feet** north of the -OHE- line.\n*   **Bottom Right Area (Southeast corner of the site):** As the plan moves eastward along the bottom boundary, there is a mix of small and large tree symbols. Near the callouts labeled \"M\", \"H.7\", and \"H.8\", there are three large tree symbols and one small tree symbol. The centers of these trees appear to be planted closer to the line, approximately **5 to 10 feet** north of the -OHE- line. The drawn mature canopy circles for the larger trees in this section heavily overlap the -OHE- line.\n\n**3. Areas Where Trees Are Shown in Proximity to Overhead Electric Facilities**\nThe primary area of conflict or proximity is the **entire southern landscape buffer** running along the bottom property line. Specifically, the large canopy trees proposed near the southeast corner (bottom right of the drawing, near the \"H.8\" callout) are shown with center points planted very close to the -OHE- line, with their mature canopies drawn directly over the utility line path.\n\n**4. OHE Characteristics (Distribution vs. Transmission)**\nBased on the visual characteristics depicted on the plan, these lines appear to be **distribution** lines rather than transmission lines.\n\n*   **Context:** The lines are running immediately adjacent to the property boundary in what appears to be a standard street right-of-way bordering a townhome development.\n*   **Pole Spacing and Footprint:** The symbols indicating poles (small crossed circles) are relatively close together. Using the 20' scale bar, the distance between the visible pole on the far left and the next pole to its right is roughly 80 to 100 feet. This shorter span distance, combined with small point symbols for the poles rather than large tower footprints, is characteristic of standard urban/suburban distribution lines. Transmission lines typically require much wider dedicated easements, larger support structures, and longer spans between structures.","providerMetadata":{"google":{"thoughtSignature":""}}}],"finishReason":"stop","usage":{"inputTokens":1424,"outputTokens":781,"totalTokens":3954,"reasoningTokens":1749},"warnings":[],"request":{"body":{"prompt":[{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"file","mediaType":"image/jpeg","data":"[BINARY_DATA_REMOVED - length: 1755680]"},{"type":"text","text":"# Your task\n\nYou are analyzing a subset of a real estate development site plan submission document.\n\nVisually analyze the binary file provided with extreme accuracy. Pay close attention to tables, reproducing them EXACTLY as they appear, character-by-character.\n\nCRITICAL: Do not paraphrase, interpret, or \"fill in\" text. If you cannot read something clearly, state \"text unclear\" rather than guessing. Transcribe field labels and values exactly as written.\n\nIf the requirements being validated require certain content to be present, look closely for it and provide all relevant information that pertains to the broader request. Remember that you are only looking at a single supplementary document, or a single page from the primary site plan. Just because you do not find evidence for a requirement on this page does not mean it will not be present on another page.\n\nReport back precisely on what you see, using exact quotes for any text you transcribe from forms, tables, or labels.\n\nThe final review agent will not be able to look at this document directly, so you must be their eyes. Ensure you describe everything relevant in 