ts labeled \"M\", \"H.7\", and \"H.8\", there are three large tree symbols and one small tree symbol. The centers of these trees appear to be planted closer to the line, approximately **5 to 10 feet** north of the -OHE- line. The drawn mature canopy circles for the larger trees in this section heavily overlap the -OHE- line.\n\n**3. Areas Where Trees Are Shown in Proximity to Overhead Electric Facilities**\nThe primary area of conflict or proximity is the **entire southern landscape buffer** running along the bottom property line. Specifically, the large canopy trees proposed near the southeast corner (bottom right of the drawing, near the \"H.8\" callout) are shown with center points planted very close to the -OHE- line, with their mature canopies drawn directly over the utility line path.\n\n**4. OHE Characteristics (Distribution vs. Transmission)**\nBased on the visual characteristics depicted on the plan, these lines appear to be **distribution** lines rather than transmission lines.\n\n*   **Context:** The lines are running immediately adjacent to the property boundary in what appears to be a standard street right-of-way bordering a townhome development.\n*   **Pole Spacing and Footprint:** The symbols indicating poles (small crossed circles) are relatively close together. Using the 20' scale bar, the distance between the visible pole on the far left and the next pole to its right is roughly 80 to 100 feet. This shorter span distance, combined with small point symbols for the poles rather than large tower footprints, is characteristic of standard urban/suburban distribution lines. Transmission lines typically require much wider dedicated easements, larger support structures, and longer spans between structures."}]}
