on type\n    23→\n    24→**Content blocks on this sheet:**\n    25→\n    26→*   **Blocks 12 & 15:** Austin Water \"Suitable and Sufficient Existing Service\" approval letters for water and wastewater. READ the block content for the approved project specifications, demand calculations, and the specific existing 8-inch mains designated for the tap.\n    27→*   **Block 17:** Benchmark data. READ for exact elevations and locations to be used as control points for the site.\n    28→*   **Block 19:** Sequence of Construction. READ the block content for the strict 13-step chronological order of operations, particularly regarding when erosion controls and pond grading must occur.\n    29→*   **Block 6:** Project Legend. READ for the text descriptions of utilities, and use VISION to familiarize yourself with the symbol shapes (e.g., property lines, utility lines, tree protection zones) to interpret subsequent plan sheets accurately.\n    30→*   **Block 9:** Fire Department requirements. READ for strict design constraints regarding emergency access, pavement load ratings, and fire hydrant placement.\n    31→*   **Block 16:** Electric Transmission Notes. READ for critical safety clearances, structural load requirements for roads in easements, and barricade rules required by Austin Energy.\n    32→*   **Block 7:** Right-of-Way Management Standard Notes. READ for traffic control rules, restricted working hours, and permit violation penalties.\n    33→*   **Block 8:** General Construction Notes and Developer Information. READ to identify the property owner (WM 41 Austin RE LLC) and general engineering responsibilities.\n    34→*   **Blocks 14 & 18:** Electric Notes and Austin Energy standard requirements. READ for tree pruning, easement access, and code compliance constraints.\n    35→*   **Block 11:** Site Plan Release Notes. READ for general City of Austin demolition and utility relocation policies. \n    36→*   **Block 20:** Legal Description. READ to verify the exact lot and subdivision references (Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision).\n    37→*   **Blocks 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 10, 13:** Various administrative and boilerplate content (Title block info, Engineer's seal, Texas 811 logo, ADA disclaimers). READ the block content briefly for project tracking metadata, otherwise these can be safely skipped.\n    38→\n    39→---\n    40→\n    41→- `block-6.md` — table: Legend for existing utility and feature symbols used on the site plan.\n    42→- `block-9.md` — text_block: Standard notes for managing construction within the Right-Of-Way, covering violations, permitting, t\n    43→- `block-10.md` — text_block: List of 13 steps detailing the construction sequence, from installing temporary erosion controls to \n    44→- `block-13.md` — text_block: List of 16 notes detailing restrictions and requirements for work near electric transmission lines a\n    45→- `block-14.md` — text_block: Copy of a letter from Austin Water confirming that the existing wastewater main has adequate capacit\n    46→- `block-16.md` — text_block: Copy of a letter from Austin Water confirming that the existing water main has adequate capacity for\n    47→- `block-17.md` — text_block: Additional notes outlining requirements from Austin Energy regarding easements, tree work, erosion c\n    48→- `block-19.md` — text_block: List of 5 notes regarding electric utilities, specifically referencing Austin Energy's requirements \n    49→\n    50→All other block detail in `./blocks.md`\n    51→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"537eedd0-5217-4f75-802b-fac17558245e","uuid":"9bfc4b9f-c617-4f75-9b46-9185dcace5b2","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/vercel/sandbox/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-03/guide.md","content":"# Sheet 3 — General Notes\n\nComprehensive general notes, sequence of construction, and project legend.\n\n## Reading Guide\n\nThis sheet provides a comprehensive collection of general notes, standard specifications, utility capacity approvals, and the site's legend for the Valley View Townhomes project. Its primary purpose is to establish project-wide regulatory constraints, outline the construction sequence, and document the City of Austin's confirmation of sufficient water and wastewater service.\n\n**Key values:**\n*   Project Scope: 39 Condo Units, 28 Living Unit Equivalents (LUE), 3.59 Acres\n*   Water Demand: 60 gpm (Estimated Peak Hour), requiring connection to existing 8-inch main\n*   Fire Flow Requirement: 1,500 gpm\n*   Wastewater Flow: 20 gpm (Estimated Peak Wet Weather), requiring connection to existing 8-inch gravity main\n*   Benchmark 1 (Mag Nail in Asphalt): Elevation = 678.52' NAVD 88\n*   Benchmark 2 (Mag Nail in Asphalt): Elevation = 681.08' NAVD 88\n*   Fire Department Paving Requirement: 80,000 lb. live-vehicle load capability\n*   Fire Apparatus Vertical Clearance: 14 feet\n*   Fire Hydrant Placement: Center of 4-inch opening must be at least 18 inches above finished grade\n*   Electric Transmission Clearances: No digging/grading/trees within 25 feet of transmission structures; 20-foot clearance for aerial equipment/staging\n*   Transmission Easement Road Requirement: Must support a 46,000 lb. tandem axle\n*   ROW Work Zone Peak Hours: 6:00 a.m. to 9:00 a.m. and 4:00 p.m. to 6:00 p.m.\n*   ROW Violation Fee: $250 per specific violation type\n\n**Content blocks on this sheet:**\n\n*   **Blocks 12 & 15:** Austin Water \"Suitable and Sufficient Existing Service\" approval letters for water and wastewater. READ the block content for the approved project specifications, demand calculations, and the specific existing 8-inch mains designated for the tap.\n*   **Block 17:** Benchmark data. READ for exact elevations and locations to be used as control points for the site.\n*   **Block 19:** Sequence of Construction. READ the block content for the strict 13-step chronological order of operations, particularly regarding when erosion controls and pond grading must occur.\n*   **Block 6:** Project Legend. READ for the text descriptions of utilities, and use VISION to familiarize yourself with the symbol shapes (e.g., property lines, utility lines, tree protection zones) to interpret subsequent plan sheets accurately.\n*   **Block 9:** Fire Department requirements. READ for strict design constraints regarding emergency access, pavement load ratings, and fire hydrant placement.\n*   **Block 16:** Electric Transmission Notes. READ for critical safety clearances, structural load requirements for roads in easements, and barricade rules required by Austin Energy.\n*   **Block 7:** Right-of-Way Management Standard Notes. READ for traffic control rules, restricted working hours, and permit violation penalties.\n*   **Block 8:** General Construction Notes and Developer Information. READ to identify the property owner (WM 41 Austin RE LLC) and general engineering responsibilities.\n*   **Blocks 14 & 18:** Electric Notes and Austin Energy standard requirements. READ for tree pruning, easement access, and code compliance constraints.\n*   **Block 11:** Site Plan Release Notes. READ for general City of Austin demolition and utility relocation policies. \n*   **Block 20:** Legal Description. READ to verify the exact lot and subdivision references (Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision).\n*   **Blocks 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 10, 13:** Various administrative and boilerplate content (Title block info, Engineer's seal, Texas 811 logo, ADA disclaimers). READ the block content briefly for project tracking metadata, otherwise these can be safely skipped.\n\n---\n\n- `block-6.md` — table: Legend for existing utility and feature symbols used on the site plan.\n- `block-9.md` — text_block: Standard notes for managing construction within the Right-Of-Way, covering violations, permitting, t\n- `block-10.md` — text_block: List of 13 steps detailing the construction sequence, from installing temporary erosion controls to \n- `block-13.md` — text_block: List of 16 notes detailing restrictions and requirements for work near electric transmission lines a\n- `block-14.md` — text_block: Copy of a letter from Austin Water confirming that the existing wastewater main has adequate capacit\n- `block-16.md` — text_block: Copy of a letter from Austin Water confirming that the existing water main has adequate capacity for\n- `block-17.md` — text_block: Additional notes outlining requirements from Austin Energy regarding easements, tree work, erosion c\n- `block-19.md` — text_block: List of 5 notes regarding electric utilities, specifically referencing Austin Energy's requirements \n\nAll other block detail in `./blocks.md`\n","numLines":51,"startLine":1,"totalLines":51}}}
{"level":30,"time":1778246333864,"pid":122,"hostname":"bd047d16-36d","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":4,"runIndex":"run-2","documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNum":9,"prompt":"This is the main site plan. Please answer:\n1. Are overhead electric (OHE) lines shown on this plan? If yes, describe their location relative to the property boundary and buildings.\n2. Are they existing utilities outside the property or proposed utilities within/crossing the property?\n3. Are there any utility easements related to electric infrastructure shown?\n4. Describe the overall orientation and layout of the site relative to Valley View Road.\n5. Are any transformers, pull boxes, or other electric equipment shown on the site?","res":{"steps":[{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on a visual analysis of the provided site plan document, here are the answers to your questions:\n\n**1. Are overhead electric (OHE) lines shown on this plan? If yes, describe their location relative to the property boundary and buildings.**\nYes, overhead electric lines are shown on this plan. They are represented by a dashed line type interspersed with the text \"**-OHE-**\".\n*   **Location relative to property boundary:** The OHE line runs roughly parallel to the southern property boundary. It appears to be located just outside (south of) the heavy solid line that delineates the main property boundary.\n*   **Location relative to buildings:** The OHE line is situated south of the buildings in the lower portion of the site (specifically south of Bldg. 8, Bldg. 7, and Bldg. 6). It is separated from the buildings by the internal drive aisle, parking spaces, and concrete retaining walls.\n\n**2. Are they existing utilities outside the property or proposed utilities within/crossing the property?**\nBased on the visual evidence, they appear to be **existing utilities located outside the property**. The \"-OHE-\" line runs externally to the heavy solid black line that denotes the perimeter property boundary along the southern edge. Furthermore, OHE lines denoted in this manner on site plans typically represent existing infrastructure constraints rather than proposed new overhead lines (which are generally placed underground in new developments unless specified otherwise).\n\n**3. Are there any utility easements related to electric infrastructure shown?**\nYes, there are easements related to electric infrastructure explicitly labeled on the plan:\n*   In the bottom right (southeast) corner of the property boundary, there is a label pointing to a dashed line area that reads: \"**5' Elec. & Tele. Easement Vol. 5468, Pg. 883 D.R.T.C.Tx.**\"\n*   Additionally, along the northern boundary near the center-right, there is a label for a \"**5' P.U.E.**\" (Public Utility Easement, which typically encompasses electric infrastructure), referencing \"Vol. 73, Pg. 76 P.R.T.C.Tx.\"\n\n**4. Describe the overall orientation and layout of the site relative to Valley View Road.**\n*   **Orientation:** The North arrow (located in the bottom right corner of the site map area, pointing straight up) indicates standard orientation where North is at the top of the page. Valley View Road runs vertically along the **western boundary** (left side) of the site.\n*   **Layout:** The site is accessed directly from Valley View Road via two proposed driveways on the west (labeled \"Construct Type II Driveway\"). The interior layout consists of eight multi-family residential buildings (Bldg. 1 through Bldg. 8) arranged centrally in roughly two east-west rows. A main internal drive aisle loops around these buildings to provide access to parking spaces. A detention pond (labeled \"Detention Pond\") is situated in the northeast corner of the site.\n\n**5. Are any transformers, pull boxes, or other electric equipment shown on the site?**\nYes, specific electric equipment is shown on the site plan:\n*   There is a solid rectangular feature explicitly labeled \"**Transformer Pad (Typ.)**\". It is located on the western side of the site, situated between Building 2 and Valley View Road, nestled next to a \"Tree Well\" and a sidewalk leading to the building.\n*   No features explicitly labeled as \"pull box\" or other specific electric equipment panels are immediately apparent or legible in the provided view.","providerMetadata":{"google":{"thoughtSignature":""}}}],"finishReason":"stop","usage":{"inputTokens":1421,"outputTokens":757,"totalTokens":4696,"reasoningTokens":2518},"warnings":[],"request":{"body":{"prompt":[{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"file","mediaType":"image/jpeg","data":"[BINARY_DATA_REMOVED - length: 2702484]"},{"type":"text","text":"# Your task\n\nYou are analyzing a subset of a real estate development site plan submission document.\n\nVisually analyze the binary file provided with extreme accuracy. Pay close attention to tables, reproducing them EXACTLY as they appear, character-by-character.\n\nCRITICAL: Do not paraphrase, interpret, or \"fill in\" text. If you cannot read something clearly, state \"text unclear\" rather than guessing. Transcribe field labels and values exactly as written.\n\nIf the requirements being validated require certain content to be present, look closely for it and provide all relevant information that pertains to the broader request. Remember that you are only looking at a single supplementary document, or a single page from the primary site plan. Just because you do not find evidence for a requirement on this page does not mean it will not be present on another page.\n\nReport back precisely on what you see, using exact quotes for any text you transcribe from forms, tables, or labels.\n\nThe final review agent will not be able to look at this document directly, so you must be their eyes. Ensure you describe everything relevant in full detail with perfect accuracy.\n"},{"type":"text","text":"This is the main site plan. Please answer:\n1. Are overhead electric (OHE) lines shown on this plan? If