tions\nBecause the pads themselves are not drafted, there are no spot elevations dedicated specifically to the corners of transformer pads. However, the surrounding grade elevations in the easement areas are as follows:\n\n*   **Near Location A (East of Bldg. 6):** \n    *   Proposed contour lines **681** and **682** run between Bldg. 6 and the easement.\n    *   An existing grade spot elevation is labeled exactly as **\"EG 689.84\"** within the text of the easement label.\n    *   The top of the proposed retaining wall adjacent to this easement is labeled **\"TW 680.26\"**.\n*   **Near Location B (South of Bldg. 8):**\n    *   Finished grade spot elevations along the property line/easement area are labeled exactly as **\"FG 680.70\"**, **\"FG 681.18\"**, and **\"FG 682.12\"**.\n    *   Adjacent top/bottom of curb spot elevations between the building and the easement include **\"681.69 / 681.19\"** and **\"682.06 / 681.56\"**.\n    *   The Finished Floor Elevation (FFE) for Bldg. 8 is **685.50**.\n*   **Near Location C (Southeast Corner):**\n    *   This is an area with a steep drop-off behind existing and proposed retaining walls. An existing grade spot elevation is labeled exactly as **\"EG 668.59\"**.\n    *   An adjacent top of wall elevation is labeled **\"TW 678.50\"**.\n\n### 3. Drainage Direction\nBased on the proposed contour lines and spot elevations, the macro-drainage patterns in these easement areas are as follows:\n\n*   **Location A (East of Bldg. 6):** The land slopes downwards from West to East. Drainage flows **away** from Bldg. 6 (FFE 685.0) down across contours 682 and 681, directing water toward the eastern property line and the retaining wall.\n*   **Location B (South of Bldg. 8):** The land slopes downwards from North to South. Drainage flows **away** from Bldg. 8 (FFE 685.50), dropping to curb elevations in the ~681.69 range, and further down to finished grades in the ~680.70 range at the southern property line/retaining wall.\n\n### 4. Oil-Filled Transformer Equipment Drainage Compliance\nBecause the specific micro-grading of concrete pads is not shown on this sheet, a definitive assessment of the pad itself cannot be made. \n\nHowever, looking at the macro-grading of the electrical easement areas where these pads would likely sit (East of Bldg. 6 and South of Bldg. 8), **the general grading plan does successfully direct surface flow AWAY from the adjacent building structures** and toward the perimeter retaining walls and property lines. If oil-filled equipment were placed in these easements matching the surrounding proposed grade, liquid would flow away from the buildings.\n\n### 5. Grading Notes or Special Considerations\nThere are **no specific grading notes, callouts, or details** on Sheet 11 that mention transformers, electrical pads, or special drainage considerations for oil-filled equipment. \n\nThe only general grading note present is located in the bottom left corner under \"Curb Note\", which reads exactly: *\"Grading lines that indicate flows toward the curb shall be constructed as a catch curb. Grade lines that indicate flows away from curb shall be constructed as a spill curb.\"*"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bba534d5-0ed6-41d8-bc2c-fb63a7697ee2","uuid":"374b0b71-1e9b-4548-b7ca-6178a82c8e1c","tool_use_result":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on a meticulous visual analysis of Sheet 11 (Grading Plan) for the Valley View Townhomes project, here are the findings regarding your requests. \n\n**CRITICAL OBSERVATION:** Physical electrical transformer concrete pads (typically depicted as solid or crosshatched rectangles and explicitly labeled \"Transformer\" or \"Pad\") are **not drafted or labeled** on this specific grading plan sheet. \n\nHowever, there are explicitly labeled **electrical easements** where this equipment is typically placed. To provide you with the most relevant information possible, the analysis below is based on the locations of these labeled electrical easement