{"level":30,"time":1778246414776,"pid":122,"hostname":"bd047d16-36d","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen_01KR3VXKTASF26V14RZ1QFYE3J","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"No references to utility-compatible designation or ECM Appendix F in the main sheet blocks. The plant schedule shows species and planting heights but NOT mature heights. This is a major gap.\n\nLet me verify the specific tree species and their mature heights. According to the grouping, ECM Appendix F contains a definitive table with:\n- Utility-compatible (UC) designation in a \"UC\" column\n- Mature height (MH) classifications\n\nThe grouping specifically states:\n- Cedar Elm: This is an ECM Appendix F tree - commonly used for utility purposes\n- Live Oak: ECM Appendix F lists at 40 feet as the standard height\n- Texas Mountain Laurel: Grouping mentions this is 25 ft mature (UC-compatible)\n- Mexican Redbud: Not specifically mentioned in the grouping overview\n\nWithout access to the actual ECM Appendix F table, I need to be conservative. The key issue is:\n\n1. The plant schedule does NOT specify mature heights - only planting heights (16', 8', 10')\n2. The plant schedule does NOT reference ECM Appendix F or utility-compatible designation\n3. The landscape plan does NOT differentiate \"large trees\" (40+ ft mature height)\n\nThis creates several deficiencies:\n\n**EL-2.1: Non-utility-compatible trees within 15 ft of distribution conductor**\n- FAIL: Plant schedule does not verify UC status against ECM Appendix F\n- Applicant must verify each species' UC designation - this is not done\n- Cannot confirm compliance without this verification\n\n**EL-2.3: Large trees (40+ ft mature height) within 25 ft of distribution conductor**\n- NOT-VERIFIABLE: Mature heights not specified in plant schedule\n- Cannot determine which trees, if any, are \"large\"\n\n**EL-2.11: Large trees not differentiated**\n- NOT-VERIFIABLE: No mature heights specified, so cannot differentiate large trees\n\n\n- Plant legend and species identification are provided with both common and scientific names. Tree locations are clearly shown relative to the overhead electric line on the landscape plan. I need to check the electrical plan for transformer pads or other equipment that might trigger the 10-foot clearance requirement.","signature":""}],"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0303f2e2-ff0c-4b14-bd62-f1e72863d84c","uuid":"ba207575-ac1a-4a66-b265-d68d10b0cdaf"}
