{"level":30,"time":1778246475401,"pid":122,"hostname":"bd047d16-36d","step":"review-runs","item":"13.md","index":2,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen_01KR3VZK9HFATB6F12FHM1DHTD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WxSYSrtxXwVAfg2d8dbo3X","name":"mcp__conductor_tools__vision","input":{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNum":2,"prompt":"Examine Sheet 2 (Plat) and provide information about:\n\n1. Property boundaries and lot lines - What is the exact boundary of Lot 1? Are property lines clearly delineated?\n\n2. Easements shown - What easements are depicted on the plat? Include:\n   - Electrical easements (type, width, location)\n   - Drainage easements\n   - Water easements\n   - Wastewater easements\n   - Public utility easements\n   - Any other easements\n   \n   For each, note the width, direction, and location relative to the property lines.\n\n3. Right-of-way - Is there a public right-of-way shown for Valley View Road?\n\n4. Scale and orientation - Confirm the scale noted on the plat and the orientation (north arrow if present).\n\n5. Notes and declarations - Are there any legal notes, dedications, or restrictions noted on the plat that affect the property?\n\nThis is the historical plat from the Mecey Subdivision (1975), so these easements may be foundational to the current site."}}],"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bba534d5-0ed6-41d8-bc2c-fb63a7697ee2","uuid":"45ec059f-6d0b-4eee-8f7f-220a360e8f4d"}
{"level":30,"time":1778246475439,"pid":122,"hostname":"bd047d16-36d","step":"review-runs","item":"13.md","index":2,"runIndex":"run-1","documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNum":2,"prompt":"Examine Sheet 2 (Plat) and provide information about:\n\n1. Property boundaries and lot lines - What is the exact boundary of Lot 1? Are property lines clearly delineated?\n\n2. Easements shown - What easements are depicted on the plat? Include:\n   - Electrical easements (type, width, location)\n   - Drainage easements\n   - Water easements\n   - Wastewater easements\n   - Public utility easements\n   - Any other easements\n   \n   For each, note the width, direction, and location relative to the property lines.\n\n3. Right-of-way - Is there a public right-of-way shown for Valley View Road?\n\n4. Scale and orientation - Confirm the scale noted on the plat and the orientation (north arrow if present).\n\n5. Notes and declarations - Are there any legal notes, dedications, or restrictions noted on the plat that affect the property?\n\nThis is the historical plat from the Mecey Subdivision (1975), so these easements may be foundational to the current site.","msg":"Calling vision tool: 1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777 (2)"}
{"level":30,"time":1778246481319,"pid":122,"hostname":"bd047d16-36d","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":4,"runIndex":"run-2","type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen_01KR3VXV7XWXV21ZR2EVJN6DWX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NG9rzHfx8TLuz6MJPHBtW5","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"grouping":"2","findings":[{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.1","status":"not-verifiable","codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.10.4"],"applicableAreas":["Southern property boundary along OHE line"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"Non-utility-compatible trees may be proposed within 15 lateral feet of the overhead electric distribution conductor along the southern boundary. However, species cannot be verified from the landscape plan symbols, which show only generic tree circles labeled with category codes (M, PL, SYM) with no reference to the plant schedule species. Distance from conductor centerline to tree trunk cannot be verified due to lack of conductor position markings and clearance dimensions on the plan. Applicant must provide: (1) species identification for each tree symbol on landscape plan cross-referenced to plant schedule; (2) verification of UC status from ECM Appendix F for each species; (3) measured clearance dimensions from outside conductor (not pole center) to tree trunk center.","agentTrace":{"observation":"The landscape plan (Sheet 31) shows trees proposed along the southern property boundary with the OHE line also depicted. The plant schedule (Sheet 35) lists specific tree species with sizes, but the landscape plan symbols do not identify species—they only show generic circles with category labels (M, PL). No clearance dimensions or conductor positions are marked on the landscape plan.","reasoning":"Since tree species cannot be identified from the landscape plan symbols themselves, compliance with the non-UC tree clearance requirement cannot be verified. The grouping requires species identification on landscape plans to determine UC status. Additionally, no clearance measurements are provided, preventing verification of the 15-foot distance threshold. Without both species identification and measured clearances, the status must be not-verifiable rather than fail.","tools_used":["vision"]}},{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.2","status":"not-verifiable","codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.10.4"],"applicableAreas":["Southern property boundary along OHE line"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31},{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":9}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"Compliance with transmission conductor clearance requirements cannot be verified because the voltage classification of the overhead electric line is not specified on any submitted plan. Per the grouping regulatory overview, transmission facilities are energized at 60kV or higher; most overhead facilities on typical development sites are distribution (less than 60kV). Without voltage documentation, it cannot be determined whether the 50-foot transmission clearance requirement applies. Additionally, tree species cannot be identified from landscape plan symbols (see EL-2.1). Applicant must provide: (1) voltage classification of the OHE line; (2) species identification keyed to landscape plan symbols; (3) measured clearance distances if transmission.","agentTrace":{"observation":"The electrical design plan (Sheet 21) and site plan (Sheet 9) show an existing OHE line but provide no voltage label or specification. The landscape plan shows trees near this line but provides no species identification on the tree symbols and no clearance dimensions.","reasoning":"The 50-foot transmission clearance rule only applies if the OHE is a transmission facility (≥60kV). The plans do not specify the voltage, so transmission applicability cannot be determined. Additionally, tree species cannot be verified from the landscape plan symbols alone. Without voltage classification and species identification, compliance cannot be verified.","tools_used":["vision"]}},{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.3","status":"not-verifiable","codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.10.4"],"applicableAreas":["Southern property boundary and western areas near transformer pad"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31},{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":35}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"Large trees (mature height 40+ feet per ECM Appendix F) may be proposed within 25 lateral feet of the overhead electric distribution conductor. The plant schedule (Sheet 35) lists species with sizes (Cedar Elm, Live Oak), but the landscape plan (Sheet 31) does not differentiate trees by size or mature height. Tree symbols on the landscape plan are shown only as generic circles with category labels (M, PL, SYM), with no notation of which trees are 'large' per ECM Appendix F standards. Measured clearance distances from conductor to tree trunks are also not provided. Applicant must provide: (1) identification of each tree symbol on landscape plan keyed to plant schedule; (2) mature height notation for each tree per ECM Appendix F; (3) measured clearance distances from outside conductor to tree trunk center for trees within 25 feet.","agentTrace":{"observation":"Sheet 35 lists tree species and specs (e.g., Cedar Elm 100 gal, 4\" cal). Sheet 31 landscape plan shows tree symbols without size differentiation or species identification. No clearance dimensions appear on Sheet 31.","reasoning":"The landscape plan does not differentiate large trees from small trees—all are shown as generic circles. Without visual identification on the landscape plan of which trees are 40+ feet, and without measured distances to the OHE, compliance cannot be verified. The grouping requires large trees to be differentiated on plans (EL-2.11), and this deficiency prevents evaluation of the 25-foot threshold.","tools_used":["vision"]}},{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.4","status":"not-verifiable","codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.10.4"],"applicableAreas":["Southern property boundary along OHE line"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"Large trees (mature height 40+ feet) may be proposed within 50 lateral feet of overhead electric transmission conductor. Compliance cannot be verified due to three factors: (1) OHE voltage not specified, so transmission applicability unknown; (2) landscape plan does not differentiate tree sizes or show mature heights; (3) clearance distances from conductor not measured. Applicant must provide: (1) OHE voltage classification; (2) mature height notation for trees on landscape plan keyed to ECM Appendix F; (3) measured clearances if transmission voltage confirmed.","agentTrace":{"observation":"Same situation as EL-2.3: landscape plan shows generic tree symbols without size notation, and OHE voltage is unspecified. Vision analysis of Sheet 31 confirmed that tree symbols do not show mature heights or differentiation.","reasoning":"Transmission applicability is unknown (voltage unspecified), and large trees are not differentiated on the landscape plan. Three independent reasons prevent verification: voltage unknown, tree sizes not shown, distances not measured.","tools_used":["vision"]}},{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.5","status":"not-verifiable","codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.10.4","UCM § 1.14.6"],"applicableAreas":["Potential transmission easement areas on site"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":11},{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"Trees may be proposed within transmission easement boundaries. Compliance cannot be verified because transmission easement boundaries are not clearly shown or labeled on the landscape plan (Sheet 31). The grading plan (Sheet 11) indicates that easements (including electric easements) are depicted, but the specific type (transmission vs. distribution), location, and boundaries are not clearly marked with dimensions or notes tied to the landscape plan. The existing OHE line is shown as a dashed line on Sheet 31 but is not explicitly identified as being within or outside a transmission easement boundary. Applicant must provide: (1) transmission easement boundaries clearly identified and dimensioned on landscape plan; (2) verification that no trees are shown within any transmission easement.","agentTrace":{"observation":"Sheet 11 (grading plan) shows easements are depicted including electric easements, but Sheet 31 (landscape plan) does not clearly label transmission easement boundaries. The OHE line is shown but not linked to a specific easement boundary designation.","reasoning":"Without clear, dimensioned easement boundary depictions on the landscape plan, it cannot be determined whether trees are within or outside transmission easement areas. The grouping requires verification that no trees are shown within transmission easement boundaries, but the boundaries are not sufficiently identified.","tools_used":["vision"]}},{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.6","status":"not-verifiable","codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.10.4"],"applicableAreas":["Site perimeter and Valley View Road frontage"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"Compliance with the 10-foot utility pole clearance requirement cannot be verified because utility poles are not shown on the landscape plan (Sheet 31). Vision analysis confirmed that the OHE line is depicted but no pole locations or symbols are visible on the landscape plan. The electrical design plan (Sheet 21) notes an existing OHE line on the western edge outside the property boundary, but does not show pole locations within or adjacent to the development site. Applicant must provide: (1) identification of all existing and proposed overhead electric utility pole locations shown on site plan or utility plan; (2) landscape plan showing pole locations if any exist within or adjacent to development site; (3) measured clearances from trees to pole centers if poles are present.","agentTrace":{"observation":"Vision analysis of the landscape plan (Sheet 31) found the OHE line depicted but no utility pole symbols visible. The electrical plan (Sheet 21) notes an existing OHE outside the property but does not show pole locations on the site.","reasoning":"Without utility pole locations shown on the landscape plan, it cannot be determined whether trees violate the 10-foot clearance. Poles may exist outside the property boundary (e.g., in Valley View Road ROW), but they are not identified or dimensioned on the submitted landscape plan.","tools_used":["vision"]}},{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.7","status":"not-verifiable","codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.10.4"],"applicableAreas":["Western site area near transformer pad between Building 2 and Valley View Road"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":9},{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"Trees may be proposed within 10 lateral feet of the transformer pad (pad-mounted electric equipment) shown on Sheet 9 near the western site boundary between Building 2 and Valley View Road. Vision analysis of the landscape plan (Sheet 31) indicates trees in close proximity to a 'rectangular pad structure' in this area. However, compliance cannot be verified because: (1) clearance distances from the transformer pad to tree trunks are not dimensioned on the landscape plan; (2) the exact location and footprint of the transformer pad is not referenced or dimensioned on the landscape plan; (3) no notation indicates access door orientation for pad-mounted equipment. Applicant must provide: (1) transformer pad location and footprint clearly marked on landscape plan with dimensions; (2) measured clearance distances from pad perimeter (and access door area if applicable) to each tree within 25 feet; (3) verification of 10-foot minimum clearance.","agentTrace":{"observation":"Sheet 9 shows a transformer pad near Building 2 and Valley View Road. Sheet 31 vision analysis noted trees 'in close proximity' to a 'rectangular pad structure' in the western area, but no clearance dimensions or pad outline appear on the landscape plan.","reasoning":"The transformer pad location is confirmed on the site plan, and trees are shown near it on the landscape plan, but clearance distances are not dimensioned. Without measured distances, compliance with the 10-foot requirement cannot be verified.","tools_used":["vision"]}},{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.8","status":"not-verifiable","codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.10.4"],"applicableAreas":["Entire site area with underground electrical infrastructure"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":21},{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"Trees are proposed throughout the site where underground electrical infrastructure (transformers, pull boxes, service lines) exists. Per Sheet 21, multiple underground transformer pads and pull boxes are located throughout the development serving each building. Sheet 31 shows trees distributed around the perimeter and near buildings, but clearance distances from underground equipment to tree locations are not dimensioned. The 5-foot minimum clearance requirement cannot be verified because: (1) underground equipment locations are not cross-referenced or overlay on the landscape plan; (2) no clearance dimensions are provided; (3) tree symbol locations cannot be precisely related to underground utility coordinates. Applicant must provide: (1) overlay of underground electric equipment locations (transformer pads, pull boxes) on landscape plan with coordinates; (2) measured clearance distances from each tree to nearest underground equipment; (3) verification that all trees maintain minimum 5-foot lateral clearance from underground equipment.","agentTrace":{"observation":"Sheet 21 shows multiple underground electrical transformer pads and pull boxes throughout the site. Sheet 31 shows trees proposed throughout the perimeter and site areas, but the two plans are not overlaid to show relationships. No clearance dimensions appear on Sheet 31.","reasoning":"Without an overlay of underground equipment locations on the landscape plan and without measured clearances, compliance with the 5-foot threshold cannot be verified. The underground electrical infrastructure and tree locations exist on separate plans without cross-reference.","tools_used":["vision"]}},{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.9","status":"not-verifiable","codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.10.4"],"applicableAreas":["Site areas with underground electrical equipment"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31},{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":35}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"Root barriers (minimum 4 feet deep, positioned 5 feet from equipment) are required for trees proposed within 20 lateral feet of underground electric equipment. Compliance cannot be verified for multiple reasons: (1) underground equipment locations are not clearly mapped on the landscape plan (see EL-2.8); (2) no clearance dimensions identify which trees are within the 20-foot zone; (3) landscape plan details and notes do not specify root barrier requirements or details; (4) tree locations cannot be precisely measured against underground equipment coordinates. Applicant must provide: (1) landscape plan identifying which trees are within 20 feet of underground equipment; (2) specification notes requiring 4-foot deep root barriers positioned 5 feet from equipment for affected trees; (3) landscape details showing root barrier installation specifications; (4) measured clearances documenting placement.","agentTrace":{"observation":"Sheet 35 lists trees to be planted but does not show which trees are near underground equipment. Sheet 31 shows tree symbols but does not overlay underground equipment or reference root barrier requirements. No root barrier specifications appear in the reviewed landscape plan blocks.","reasoning":"Without identification of which trees are within 20 feet of underground equipment, and without root barrier specifications tied to those trees, compliance cannot be verified. The two prerequisites (distance identification and barrier specification) are both missing.","tools_used":["vision"]}},{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.10","status":"fail","codeCitations":["Reviewer Convention"],"applicableAreas":["Landscape plan (Sheet 31)"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31},{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":35}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"The landscape plan (Sheet 31) does not identify the species of trees proposed near electric facilities. The plant schedule on Sheet 35 clearly lists tree species (Cedar Elm, Live Oak, Texas Mountain Laurel, Mexican Redbud) with botanical names and specifications. However, the landscape plan symbols show only generic circles with category labels (M=mitigation, PL=parking lot, SYM) with no reference to the plant schedule or species identification. The tree symbols cannot be traced to specific species. This violates the requirement that trees shown on plans be identified. Applicant must provide: (1) landscape plan tree symbols keyed to plant schedule with species callouts; (2) for trees near electric facilities, species label on each symbol or clear legend cross-referencing symbols to species names.","agentTrace":{"observation":"Sheet 35 contains a detailed plant schedule naming all tree species. Sheet 31 (landscape plan) shows tree symbols throughout the site, but none of the symbols are labeled with species names or abbreviations, and there is no legend cross-referencing symbol types to species in the plant schedule.","reasoning":"The grouping's checklist explicitly states that the 'plant legend or plant schedule does not identify species of trees proposed near electric facilities' is a deficiency. Although the plant schedule exists elsewhere, the landscape plan itself does not identify species at the point of observation—the tree symbols lack species identification. This is a fail, not a not-verifiable, because the requirement is simply not met on the primary document where trees are located.","tools_used":["vision"]}},{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.11","status":"fail","codeCitations":["Reviewer Convention"],"applicableAreas":["Landscape plan (Sheet 31)"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31},{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":35}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"Large trees (mature height 40+ feet per ECM Appendix F) are not differentiated on the landscape plan to allow verification of clearances from overhead electric facilities. The plant schedule on Sheet 35 lists mature heights (e.g., Cedar Elm, Live Oak specifications with container sizes indicating mature trees), but the landscape plan (Sheet 31) does not show these heights or indicate which tree symbols represent large trees. All tree symbols appear as identical generic circles with only category labels (M, PL), providing no visual differentiation of size. Without height notation on the landscape plan, reviewers and inspectors cannot identify which trees are subject to the stricter 25-foot (distribution) or 50-foot (transmission) large-tree clearance requirements. Applicant must provide: (1) notation of mature height on each tree symbol on landscape plan, or (2) distinct symbol types or colors to differentiate large trees (40+ feet) from other trees, or (3) clear legend showing which species in the plant schedule meet the large-tree threshold and marking those trees accordingly on the landscape plan.","agentTrace":{"observation":"Sheet 35 plant schedule lists species and sizes (e.g., 'Cedar Elm 100 gal., 4\" cal., 16' HT. minimum'). Sheet 31 shows all tree symbols as generic circles without height notation or differentiation. Vision analysis confirmed no size or height labeling on individual tree symbols.","reasoning":"The grouping defines 'large trees' as those with mature height 40+ feet per ECM Appendix F. The landscape plan must show which trees are large to enable clearance verification. Since no trees are marked as large or shown with heights on the landscape plan, this deficiency is present. This is a fail, not a not-verifiable, because the plan element (height notation or differentiation) is simply missing.","tools_used":["vision"]}},{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.12","status":"not-verifiable","codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.10.4"],"applicableAreas":["Trees near overhead electric facilities"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"Tree clearance measurements are not dimensioned on the landscape plan, preventing evaluation of measurement reference points. The grouping's measurement methodology requires that clearances be measured from the outside conductor (not pole centerline), which can be 4+ feet offset from the pole center due to cross arms. Without clearance dimensions shown on the plan, it cannot be verified whether any measurements that may have been made used the correct reference point (outside conductor vs. pole center). This item can only be evaluated if clearance dimensions are provided on submitted plans. Applicant must provide: (1) clearance dimensions from tree trunks to outside conductor locations (not pole centerline) for any trees shown within 25 feet of electric conductors.","agentTrace":{"observation":"Vision analysis of Sheet 31 explicitly found: 'No dimension lines or specific clearance measurements (e.g., \"10' clearance\") are shown between the tree symbols and the utility lines.' The landscape plan contains no dimensional annotations for tree-to-conductor distances.","reasoning":"Since no clearance dimensions are provided on the landscape plan, there is nothing to evaluate regarding measurement reference points (conductor vs. pole center). The deficiency would be present if any dimensions were shown incorrectly, but the absence of dimensions prevents verification of either compliance or violation of this standard.","tools_used":["vision"]}},{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.13","status":"not-verifiable","codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.10.4"],"applicableAreas":["Trees near overhead electric facilities"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"Tree clearance measurements are not dimensioned on the landscape plan, preventing evaluation of tree reference points. The grouping's measurement methodology requires that clearances be measured from tree trunk center (planting location), not from canopy edge. Without clearance dimensions shown on the plan, it cannot be verified whether measurements (if provided elsewhere) reference the correct tree location point. This item can only be evaluated if clearance dimensions appear on submitted plans. Applicant must provide: (1) clearance dimensions terminating at tree trunk center (planting location symbol) for any trees shown within clearance zones of electric facilities.","agentTrace":{"observation":"Sheet 31 contains no clearance dimension lines. The vision analysis confirmed: 'No dimension lines or specific clearance measurements are shown between the tree symbols and the utility lines.' Therefore, the reference point for any tree measurement cannot be evaluated.","reasoning":"The evaluation of whether measurements reference tree trunk (correct) or canopy edge (incorrect) requires that dimensions be present on the plan. Absent dimensions, this standard cannot be evaluated. Not-verifiable is appropriate because the prerequisite (dimensioned clearances on plans) is missing.","tools_used":["vision"]}},{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.14","status":"not-verifiable","codeCitations":["Reviewer Convention"],"applicableAreas":["Landscape plan and utility infrastructure"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31},{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":9},{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":21}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"Trees are shown on the landscape plan in relation to the overhead electric facilities (OHE line is depicted on Sheet 31 along the southern boundary), but the relationship cannot be meaningfully verified due to the deficiencies in EL-2.10 and EL-2.11. Specifically: (1) trees are not identified by species on the landscape plan, preventing verification of utility compatibility; (2) trees are not differentiated by mature height, preventing identification of large-tree clearance requirements; (3) no conductor positions are marked (showing only the OHE line, not the outside conductor location offset from the pole); (4) clearance distances are not dimensioned. While trees and the OHE line are both visible on Sheet 31, sufficient cross-reference information to verify compliance with electric facility clearance requirements is not provided. Applicant must cross-reference the landscape plan to the plant schedule and provide: (1) species identification for each tree; (2) mature height notation; (3) outside conductor location and offset dimensions; (4) measured clearance distances.","agentTrace":{"observation":"Sheet 31 shows both trees (as generic circles without species labels) and the OHE line. However, the trees cannot be traced to specific species via the landscape plan itself, and the OHE line shows only the utility corridor, not conductor positions. Vision analysis found these are 'shown layout and relative positioning visually, but [lack] explicit distance measurements for utility clearances.'","reasoning":"Although trees and utilities are spatially shown together, the information necessary to verify clearance compliance (species, heights, conductor positions, measured distances) is missing. This prevents meaningful verification despite visual co-location. The deficiency is not-verifiable (rather than fail) because sufficient evidence exists to identify the problem areas—the issue is that required documentation is absent rather than documentation showing non-compliance.","tools_used":["vision"]}},{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.15","status":"not-verifiable","codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.10.4"],"applicableAreas":["Trees within potential restricted clearance zones"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"Trees appear to be proposed within prohibited clearance zones based on visual proximity to the OHE line shown on the landscape plan, but Austin Energy written approval documentation is not provided or visible on any submitted plan sheets or supplementary documents reviewed. However, definitive determination of violation cannot be made without: (1) verified species identification and utility compatibility status; (2) actual measured clearance distances; (3) OHE voltage classification (distribution vs. transmission); (4) confirmation that trees are indeed in prohibited zones per calculated thresholds. While the vision analysis indicates trees along the southern boundary have canopies that 'directly overlap or touch' the OHE line, suggesting potential violation, the absence of required documentation (species, clearances, approval) combined with unverified measurements prevents a conclusive fail determination. STATUS: Not-verifiable pending resolution of EL-2.1, EL-2.2, EL-2.3, EL-2.4 deficiencies. If any of those items prove to show trees within prohibited zones, and no Austin Energy approval is produced, this item becomes a fail.","agentTrace":{"observation":"Vision analysis stated: 'Along the southern (bottom) boundary of the site plan, the line denoting the overhead electric (\"- OHE -\") passes directly through the circular symbols representing the proposed tree canopies.' No Austin Energy approval letter or correspondence was found in the reviewed documents.","reasoning":"Trees may be in prohibited zones (apparent from visual proximity), but definitive violation requires confirmed species, measured clearances, and voltage classification. Absence of required Austin Energy approval is noted, but determination of whether approval is actually needed requires confirming that trees are in fact within prohibited zones. The status is not-verifiable because prerequisite verifications (species, clearances, voltage) are incomplete.","tools_used":["vision"]}}]}}],"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e4f0070e-2a45-4059-b037-c9cf1f4aff02","uuid":"b7247ad1-ef2a-4829-a1f4-06e8fdf78c78"}
