{"level":30,"time":1778246539211,"pid":122,"hostname":"bd047d16-36d","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":7,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0132yUTAjegWb8zFYkpcKWMk","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 3 — Block 13\n     2→\n     3→## text_block: List of 16 notes detailing restrictions and requirements for work near electric transmission lines and easements, including clearance zones, safety meetings, access, barricades, and prohibited structures.\n     4→\n     5→Electric Transmission Notes\n     6→1. No trees shall be planted within the transmission easement. No trees shall be planted within 25 feet of the base of the transmission structure. Vehicular access for Austin Energy trucks and equipment is always to be maintained within the easement. Austin Energy will not be responsible for damage and/or removal of vegetation within the easement.\n     7→2. Do not dig or grade within 25 feet of the transmission structures, including down guy anchors. Grading near electric transmission facilities must be coordinated with Austin Energy prior to commencement of grading. Call Andrew Perez at 512-505-7153 to schedule a meeting.\n     8→3. A pre-construction safety meeting is required with Austin Energy 48 hours before commencement of construction. Call Andrew Perez at 512-505-7153 to schedule a tailgate safety meeting. Include Chuck Hendry (ph 505-7151) in the meeting, if cranes are being used during construction. OSHA requires a 20' clearance from energized transmission lines during construction.\n     9→4. Barricades must be erected 10 feet from electric transmission structures during construction.\n    10→5. Any relocation of electric transmission facilities or outages caused by this project will be charged to the property owner and contractor.\n    11→6. Warning signs must be placed under the overhead electric transmission facilities as notification of the electrical hazard.\n    12→7. For safety reasons, aerial equipment, dumpsters, staging or spoils areas are not permitted within 20 feet of the transmission wire and/or structures and must be located outside the easement.\n    13→8. 24-hour access to electric facilities shall be maintained.\n    14→9. Any temporary or permanent fence preventing access to the transmission facilities and/or easement shall be coordinated with Austin Energy staff. AE staff will install a lock on the gate for access.\n    15→10. Property owner and contractor are responsible for dust controls to minimize contamination of wire and insulators caused by dust from this project. Any subsequent cleaning or electrical outages caused by dust from this project will be charged to the property owner and contractor.\n    16→11. Property owner is responsible for any damages to curbing, landscaping, walls, paving placed around the electric transmission structures/poles/lines caused by Austin Energy during maintenance and repairs. All curbing within the electric transmission easement must be laydown curbing.\n    17→12. Roads/driveways/pavement/parking within the easement should be built to support a 46,000 lb tandem axle (5 foot spacing) to ensure safety.\n    18→13. Fire hydrants must be located out of the transmission easement and a minimum of 20 feet from any transmission structure.\n    19→14. Sprinklers are prohibited within 25 feet of transmission poles and structures\n    20→15. Do not dig or grade within 25 feet of the transmission structures, including down guy anchors)\n    21→16. Owner may not place, erect, construct or maintain the following within electric transmission easement:\n    22→  · any permanent structures, including, but not limited to habitable structures such as homes, mobile homes, garages, or offices,\n    23→  · any structure of any kind in such proximity to the electric transmission or distribution lines, poles, structures, towers, or appurtenant facilities as would constitute a violation of the National Electric Safety Code in effect at the time the structure is erected, nor\n    24→  · any structures, including but not limited to, fences, storage sheds, drainage, filtration or detention ponds which would impair Austin Energy's access to the transmission easements or its lines, poles, structures, towers or appurtenant facilities in the easements.\n    25→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4b3284e2-1747-4d44-a558-2f00d9a24819","uuid":"91a7ce66-da7a-4208-ba43-eb20831e658c","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/vercel/sandbox/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-03/block-13.md","content":"# Sheet 3 — Block 13\n\n## text_block: List of 16 notes detailing restrictions and requirements for work near electric transmission lines and easements, including clearance zones, safety meetings, access, barricades, and prohibited structures.\n\nElectric Transmission Notes\n1. No trees shall be planted within the transmission easement. No trees shall be planted within 25 feet of the base of the transmission structure. Vehicular access for Austin Energy trucks and equipment is always to be maintained within the easement. Austin Energy will not be responsible for damage and/or removal of vegetation within the easement.\n2. Do not dig or grade within 25 feet of the transmission structures, including down guy anchors. Grading near electric transmission facilities must be coordinated with Austin Energy prior to commencement of grading. Call Andrew Perez at 512-505-7153 to schedule a meeting.\n3. A pre-construction safety meeting is required with Austin Energy 48 hours before commencement of construction. Call Andrew Perez at 512-505-7153 to schedule a tailgate safety meeting. Include Chuck Hendry (ph 505-7151) in the meeting, if cranes are being used during construction. OSHA requires a 20' clearance from energized transmission lines during construction.\n4. Barricades must be erected 10 feet from electric transmission structures during construction.\n5. Any relocation of electric transmission facilities or outages caused by this project will be charged to the property owner and contractor.\n6. Warning signs must be placed under the overhead electric transmission facilities as notification of the electrical hazard.\n7. For safety reasons, aerial equipment, dumpsters, staging or spoils areas are not permitted within 20 feet of the transmission wire and/or structures and must be located outside the easement.\n8. 24-hour access to electric facilities shall be maintained.\n9. Any temporary or permanent fence preventing access to the transmission facilities and/or easement shall be coordinated with Austin Energy staff. AE staff will install a lock on the gate for access.\n10. Property owner and contractor are responsible for dust controls to minimize contamination of wire and insulators caused by dust from this project. Any subsequent cleaning or electrical outages caused by dust from this project will be charged to the property owner and contractor.\n11. Property owner is responsible for any damages to curbing, landscaping, walls, paving placed around the electric transmission structures/poles/lines caused by Austin Energy during maintenance and repairs. All curbing within the electric transmission easement must be laydown curbing.\n12. Roads/driveways/pavement/parking within the easement should be built to support a 46,000 lb tandem axle (5 foot spacing) to ensure safety.\n13. Fire hydrants must be located out of the transmission easement and a minimum of 20 feet from any transmission structure.\n14. Sprinklers are prohibited within 25 feet of transmission poles and structures\n15. Do not dig or grade within 25 feet of the transmission structures, including down guy anchors)\n16. Owner may not place, erect, construct or maintain the following within electric transmission easement:\n  · any permanent structures, including, but not limited to habitable structures such as homes, mobile homes, garages, or offices,\n  · any structure of any kind in such proximity to the electric transmission or distribution lines, poles, structures, towers, or appurtenant facilities as would constitute a violation of the National Electric Safety Code in effect at the time the structure is erected, nor\n  · any structures, including but not limited to, fences, storage sheds, drainage, filtration or detention ponds which would impair Austin Energy's access to the transmission easements or its lines, poles, structures, towers or appurtenant facilities in the easements.\n","numLines":25,"startLine":1,"totalLines":25}}}
{"level":30,"time":1778246543298,"pid":122,"hostname":"bd047d16-36d","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":7,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen_01KR3W1P3R6X4MSQKS3FDXW50F","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Excellent! Block 13 contains the Electric Transmission Notes, and note #1 is critical:\n\n\"No trees shall be planted within the transmission easement. No trees shall be planted within 25 feet of the base of the transmission structure.\"\n\nThis confirms that:\n1. There ARE transmission structures on or near the property\n2. There is a categorical prohibition on planting trees within 25 feet of transmission structures\n\nNow, let me check if the OHE line shown on the landscape plan is transmission or distribution, and whether the three trees in the southeast corner are within the 25-foot transmission clearance zone or the transmission easement.\n\nThe transmission notes specify \"25 feet of the base of the transmission structure\" for the planting prohibition. The landscape plan showed the three trees in the southeast corner at 2-5 feet from the OHE line, which would definitely violate the 25-foot prohibition if this is a transmission line.\n\nHowever, I also need to determine whether the OHE line is transmission or distribution. Distribution lines typically have lower voltage and smaller structures, while transmission lines are larger. The grading plan mentioned electric easements, but didn't specify if they were transmission or distribution.\n\nLet me check block-17 and block-19 for more information about the transmission/distribution distinction.","signature":""}],"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4b3284e2-1747-4d44-a558-2f00d9a24819","uuid":"74863ec1-1843-4d17-b45f-da54bc3099c9"}
{"level":30,"time":1778246543654,"pid":122,"hostname":"bd047d16-36d","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen_01KR3W1MTYWQYNRX2QWK72W5N7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Perfect! I've completed the evaluation of all 15 checklist items for grouping EL-2 (Tree Clearances from Overhead Electric Lines). Let me summarize what I found:\n\n**SUMMARY OF FINDINGS:**\n\n**Fail (7 items):**\n- EL-2.1: Plant schedule doesn't verify utility-compatible designation against ECM Appendix F\n- EL-2.7: Transformer pads not shown on landscape plan\n- EL-2.8: Underground electrical lines not shown on landscape plan  \n- EL-2.14: Landscape plan inadequate - missing electrical infrastructure\n- EL-2.15: No Austin Energy written approval documentation for trees in restricted zones\n\n**Not-Verifiable (5 items):**\n- EL-2.3: Mature heights not specified for trees\n- EL-2.6: Utility poles not clearly identified on landscape plan\n- EL-2.9: Underground equipment not shown, so root barrier requirements cannot be verified\n- EL-2.11: Large trees cannot be differentiated without mature heights\n- EL-2.12: Clearance measurement methodology not documented\n- EL-2.13: Measurement reference point (trunk vs. canopy) not documented\n\n**Pass (1 item):**\n- EL-2.10: Species are identified by common and scientific names\n\n**N/A (3 items):**\n- EL-2.2: No transmission lines present\n- EL-2.4: No transmission lines present\n- EL-2.5: No transmission easement present\n\nThe core issues are:\n1. Inadequate coordination between landscape plan and electrical design plan\n2. Missing documentation of species utility-compatibility verification\n3. Missing mature height specifications for tree size classification\n4. Landscape plan omits electrical infrastructure (underground lines, transformer pads, poles)\n5. No evidence of Austin Energy written approval for trees in restricted zones\n\nThe review has been completed successfully and the structured output has been generated.","signature":""}],"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0303f2e2-ff0c-4b14-bd62-f1e72863d84c","uuid":"0912fe93-5ba4-496e-8ef7-47e2722fc6e5"}
