{"level":30,"time":1778246559913,"pid":122,"hostname":"bd047d16-36d","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":7,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen_01KR3W1WDP78QCDB7T6MMV2MZN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Block 19 (Electric Notes) also references overhead electric facilities and tree pruning requirements within 10 feet of centerline, but doesn't specify transmission vs. distribution.\n\nNow, based on all the information I've gathered:\n\nKey Evidence:\n1. The site plan shows transmission structures with a 25-foot prohibition on tree planting (per Block 13, Electric Transmission Notes, note #1)\n2. The landscape plan shows overhead electric (OHE) line along the southern property boundary\n3. Three mitigation trees are positioned at 2-5 feet from the OHE line per visual analysis\n4. The grading plan shows \"5' Electric Easement\" on the southern boundary (consistent with a transmission easement width)\n5. The plant schedule lists shade trees (Cedar Elm, Live Oak) which are large trees (40+ ft mature height)\n6. The landscape plan shows these three trees in the southeast corner as perfect circles (consistent with large shade trees)\n\nNow I need to determine if:\n1. The OHE line is part of the transmission system (not just distribution)\n2. The trees are actually Cedar Elm and Live Oak (large trees)\n3. Whether there's any Austin Energy approval\n\nGiven that Block 13 explicitly states \"No trees shall be planted within 25 feet of the base of the transmission structure,\" and the three trees are shown at 2-5 feet from what appears to be an OHE line with an associated 5-foot easement on the southern boundary, this appears to be a transmission structure, not a distribution line.\n\nHowever, I should note that I haven't definitively confirmed whether the OHE line is labeled as transmission or distribution in the landscape plan blocks. The vision tool said it was marked as \"-OHE-\" but didn't specify voltage or type.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, let me make my assessment based on what can be clearly verified from the plans:\n\n1. There are three trees marked \"M\" (mitigation) in the southeast corner near an overhead electric line\n\n\n2. These trees sit only 2-5 feet from the OHE line, which creates potential violations depending on line type. If distribution, large trees would violate the 15-foot clearance requirement (EL-2.1) or the 25-foot requirement for large trees (EL-2.3). If transmission, they'd violate the categorical 25-foot prohibition on tree planting near transmission structures. I need to reconsider how the transmission categorization applies—whether it's strictly about trees within transmission easement boundaries or extends to structures themselves.\n\nThe Electric Transmission Notes in Block 13 are more restrictive than the grouping's EL-2.5, stating no trees can be planted within 25 feet of transmission structure bases, regardless of easement boundaries. Meanwhile, EL-2.2 addresses non-utility-compatible trees within 50 feet of transmission conductors or equipment, which is a different standard. The discrepancy suggests the transmission notes represent Austin Energy's internal standards that may be stricter than the grouping's requirements.\n\nLooking at the site plan, three trees in the southeast corner sit 2-5 feet from an overhead electric line—well within the 15-foot threshold for non-utility-compatible trees under EL-2.1, and potentially within the 25-foot limit for large trees under EL-2.3 if the line is distribution voltage. I have enough evidence to identify violations under EL-2.1 and/or EL-2.3 based on the landscape plan's tree placement. Now I'll assess all 15 checklist items in the grouping to compile my complete findings.","signature":""}],"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4b3284e2-