Transmission easement boundaries lack clear delineation despite electrical notes referencing transmission structures on site.\n\n**Sheets:** 3, 31\n\n**Applicable Area:** Utilities & Infrastructure\n\n**Severity:** 2 (Medium) — Critical for determining applicable clearance standards but resolvable through documentation updates.\n\n**Confidence:** 2 (Medium) — Only two of three analysis runs identified these issues.\n\n**Cross-Department:** Electrical engineer and Austin Energy should verify voltage classification and confirm transmission easement boundaries.\n\n**Attention:** Runs 2 and 3 flagged these findings consistently, though run 1 did not capture them.\n\n**Details:**\n\nDetail 1 (EL-2.2):\n- Text: \"Overhead electric line voltage classification (transmission ≥60kV vs. distribution <60kV) is not specified on any submitted plan. Transmission clearance requirements (50 feet for trees, various distances for vegetation) differ from distribution requirements. Without voltage documentation, transmission rule applicability cannot be determined.\"\n- Citation: \"UCM § 1.10.10.4; ECM Appendix F\"\n- SourceID: \"2:EL-2.2\"\n- Confidence: 2/3 = **2**\n\nDetail 2 (EL-2.4):\n- Text: \"Large trees (mature height 40+ feet per ECM Appendix F) near OHE line must maintain 50-foot clearance if transmission, 25-foot clearance if distribution. Applicant must provide: (1) OHE voltage classification, (2) mature height notation for affected trees, (3) measured clearances if transmission voltage confirmed. Electrical transmission notes (Sheet 3) reference transmission structures on or adjacent to site.\"\n- Citation: \"UCM § 1.10.10.4; ECM Appendix F\"\n- SourceID: \"2:EL-2.4\"\n- Confidence: 2/3 = **2**\n\nDetail 3 (EL-2.5):\n- Text: \"Transmission easement boundaries are not clearly marked with dimensions or notes on landscape plan (Sheet 31). Grading plan (Sheet 11) indicates electric easements are depicted but type (transmission vs. distribution) and boundaries are not clearly delineated. OHE line shown as dashed line but not explicitly identified as within or outside transmission easement. Applicant must identify easement type and boundaries with clear notation on all relevant plans.\"\n- Citation: \"UCM § 1.10.10.4; ECM Appendix F\"\n- SourceID: \"2:EL-2.5\"\n- Confidence: 2/3 = **2**\n\nThe voltage classification and clearance requirements need to be established first, then easement boundaries must be clearly marked showing whether transmission or distribution applies. Sheet 3 transmission notes and Sheet 11 grading plan both reference easements but lack sufficient delineation.\n\n## COMMENT 5: Austin Energy Approval Documentation Missing for Trees in Restricted Clearance Zones\n\n**Data from findings:**\n- EL-2.15 (3 runs, f, nv, f): Landscape plan (Sheet 31) shows proposed trees located near OHE distribution line along western property boundary; no documentation of written approval from Austin Energy authorizing any trees within restricted clearance zones; Master Comment Report references indicate Austin Energy review occurred (UCM § 1.10.10.3 & 4 cited) but no approval letters or exception authorizations visible in submitted materials; trees appear to be proposed within prohibited clearance zones based on visual proximity to OHE line; three trees marked as mitigation trees in southeast corner positioned at approximately 2-5 feet from OHE line along southern property boundary; placed within restricted clearance zones (15 feet for distribution non-utility-compatible trees, 25 feet for distribution large trees, 50 feet for transmission); no documentation of Austin Energy written approval for planting trees in these locations is provided in submitted plans, application documents, or supplementary documents reviewed\n  - Sheets: 31, 35 (landscape plan and plant schedule)\n  - Area: Proposed tree locations near overhead distribution line and electrical equipment\n\nThe three mitigation trees positioned 2-5 feet from the OHE line in the southeast corner fall within prohibited clearance zones depending on species and voltage classification. No written Austin Energy approval documentation appears in the submitted materials to authorize placement in these restricted areas.\n\nI'm categorizing this under \"Utilities & Infrastructure\" since the controlling requirement is electric code compliance rather than general site layout. The severity is high—trees in restricted clearance zones without proper approval represent both a safety hazard and code violation. All three runs consistently flagged this issue with two marking it as a failure, giving me high confidence in the finding. Austin Energy must review and approve any trees proposed within these restricted zones per UCM § 1.10.10.3.\n\nThe status across runs shows some variation—one marked \"fail,\" one \"not verifiable,\" and one \"fail\"—because the actual violation depends on confirming species identification, tree sizes, and exact clearance measurements, which remain unresolved in the submission. Once those details are documented, this becomes a definitive failure. The three mitigation trees in the southeast corner sit approximately 2-5 feet from the overhead electric line, placing them well within prohibited clearance zones that require 15 feet minimum for non-utility-compatible trees, 25 feet for large distribution trees, and 50 feet for transmission lines.\n\nNo Austin Energy approval documentation appears anywhere in the submitted materials. The citation references UCM § 1.10.10.3 and § 1.10.10.4, with confidence rated at 3/3 across all runs due to the consistent flagging of missing approval documentation.\n\nNow I can verify the five comment categories capture all 15 findings. Tree species, size, and documentation issues cover EL-2.1, EL-2.10, EL-2.3, and EL-2.11. Clearance distances and measurement documentation address EL-2.12, EL-2.13, and EL-2.14. The electrical infrastructure overlay gap encompasses EL-2.6, EL-2.7, EL-2.8, and EL-2.9. Transmission line voltage and easement concerns handle EL-2.2, EL-2.4, and EL-2.5. Finally, the missing Austin Energy approval accounts for EL-2.15. That's 4 + 3 + 4 + 3 + 1 = 15 findings accounted for.\n\nThe 15 findings consolidate into a 3:1 ratio matching the target. Now I need to construct the JSON output with accurate runComments for each finding, ensuring each detail's runComments array reflects which runs flagged it. For instance, EL-2.1 was flagged in Run 1 with a fail status, and the comment notes that the plant schedule doesn't verify utility-compatible designation against ECM Appendix F, requiring documentation that non-utility-compatible species stay beyond 15 lateral feet of distribution conductors.\n\nRun 2 marked it not-verifiable because the landscape plan uses generic tree symbols without species identification, making it impossible to confirm UC status or measure clearances from the conductor. Run 3 also flagged it as not-verifiable, citing three mitigation issues that need addressing.","signature":""}],"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"68c1847e-b45d-4d71-9e30-d6d8dc76d151","uuid":"cdeae814-ee33-4bff-9ee8-2e2045f4ad81"}
{"level":30,"time":1778246871119,"pid":122,"hostname":"bd047d16-36d","step":"synthesize-comments","item":"2.json","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen_01KR3W7YZ1FZRGTS8QB6CNGY39","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Looking at this grouping of consolidated electrical infrastructure review findings, I'll analyze and synthesize them into structured comments for the review team.\n\n## Discipline & Scope Analysis\n\nThis is an **Electrical/Utility Infrastructure** review focused on **tree clearances from electric facilities** per UCM § 1.10.10.4. All 15 findings are Tier 1 (within scope) — they directly address utility clearance requirements that the utility discipline reviews and enforces.\n\n## Consolidation Strategy\n\nWith 15 input findings, I'm targeting ~4 comments (roughly 3–4 findings per comment). I've identified these thematic clusters:\n\n1. **Tree Identification & Documentation** (EL-2.1, EL-2.10, EL-2.3, EL-2.11) — Trees not identified by species or mature height\n2. **Clearance Measurement Documentation** (EL-2.12, EL-2.13, EL-2.14) — No dimensioned clearances; reference points unclear\n3. **Electrical Infrastructure Coordination** (EL-2.6, EL-2.7, EL-2.8, EL-2.9) — Poles, pads, underground lines not overlaid on landscape plan\n4. **Transmission Issues** (EL-2.2, EL-2.4, EL-2.5) — Voltage classification and easement boundaries not specified\n5. **Austin Energy Approval** (EL-2.15) — No approval documentation for trees in restricted zones\n\nThis yields 5 comments consolidating all 15 findings at approximately 3:1 ratio.\n\n---"}],"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"68c1847e-b45d-4d71-9e30-d6d8dc76d151","uuid":"b9ec74c3-05fb-4af3-97ff-6717238e3512"}
{"level":30,"time":1778246899589,"pid":122,"hostname":"bd047d16-36d","step":"synthesize-comments","item":"13.json","index":2,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen_01KR3W7YXT697F6K72KE0CZCH6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze this review comment synthesis task carefully.\n\n## Understanding the Input\n\nI have a consolidated findings grouping with 38 findings across 3 runs. The findings all relate to electrical transformer pads and associated infrastructure. The review discipline is **Utility (Electrical)** — the primary concern is transformer pad placement, clearances, easement access, and compliance with utility codes (primarily UCM = Utility Code Manual, and related ECM standards).\n\n## Step 1: Discipline Scope\n\n**Discipline:** Utility (Electrical/Transformer Infrastructure)\n\n**Primary Concern:** \n- Transformer pad placement compliance (clearances, easements, surface conditions)\n- Underground and overhead utility clearances\n- Pad accessibility, documentation, and design specifications\n- Environmental conflicts (trees, vegetation, water features)\n\nAll findings in this grouping are **Tier 1 (Within Scope)** — they directly address the electrical utility requirements and transformer placement standards. A few involve cross-departmental coordination (landscaping, grading, structural) but the core issue is utility compliance.\n\n## Step 2: Program & Overlay Hierarchy\n\nFrom the findings, the governing standards are:\n1. **UCM § 1.10.x** (Utility Code Manual, Section 1.10 — Transformer Placement Requirements)\n2. **ECM Appendix F** (Equipment Code Manual — utility-compatible tree list)\n3. **Reviewer Convention** (industry standard practices for clearances)\n\nNo project-specific variances or incentive programs mentioned. The base standard is UCM.\n\n## Step 3: Consolidation\n\nI have 38 atomic findings. I need to consolidate to roughly 38/4 = **~9-10 comments**, ideally closer to 8-10.\n\nLet me group by theme:\n\n### Group A: Transformer Type & Equipment Specs (Findings 1, 32)\n- EL-13.6: Transformer type not specified (oil-filled vs dry-type) — affects window/door clearance requirements\n\n\n- EL-13.32: Oil-filled equipment drainage direction not documented\n\nBoth address missing transformer specifications that impact design requirements — consolidate into one comment.\n\n### Group B: Building/Structure Clearances (Findings 1, 4, 5)\n- EL-13.1: Clearance from buildings not dimensioned\n- EL-13.5: No cross-section showing structures above pads\n- Multiple others: window/door/duct clearances (EL-13.6)\n\nAll relate to insufficient documentation of clearances from building elements — merge these together.\n\n### Group C: Access Door Orientation & Hot-stick Clearance (Findings 2, 14, 11)\n- EL-13.10: Hot-stick use area (access door side) requires 10-foot clearance — no door orientation shown\n- EL-13.14: Shrub/vegetation clearance from access door not verifiable without orientation\n- EL-13.11: Confined space clearance not verifiable without access door orientation\n\nThese all hinge on the same missing information: access door orientation documentation. Without knowing where the door faces, I can't verify any of the surrounding clearances.\n\n### Group D: Surrounding Infrastructure Clearances (Findings 3, 7, 8, 9, 10)\n- EL-13.12: Fire hydrant clearance (5 feet)\n- EL-13.21: Water line clearance (5 feet)\n- EL-13.22: Wastewater line clearance (5 feet)\n- EL-13.23: Storm drain clearance (5 feet) — includes one FAIL status\n- EL-13.25: Underground utility separation (12 inches)\n\nAll of these involve maintaining specific distances from utility infrastructure, making them candidates for consolidation.\n\n### Group E: Vegetation/Landscape Clearances (Findings 6, 13, 14, 15)\n- EL-13.13: Tree species identification and utility compatibility not documented\n- EL-13.14: Shrub/vegetation clearances not dimensioned\n- EL-13.15: Root barrier specifications for trees within 20 feet not shown\n- EL-13.16: Dumpster clearance (5 feet) — similar theme but dumpster is not vegetation\n- EL-13.17: Bike rack clearance (5 feet) — infrastructure, not vegetation\n- EL-13.18: Light pole clearance (5 feet) — infrastructure, not vegetation\n\nThe vegetation-specific items (tree species, shrub dimensions, root barriers) form a distinct subset, while dumpster, bike rack, and light pole clearances are really infrastructure items that don't belong in this group.\n\n### Group F: Overhead Infrastructure (Findings 27, 28, 39)\n- EL-13.27: Overhead distribution line clearance (7.5 feet horizontal)\n- EL-13.28: Overhead distribution conductor clearance (15-foot radius)\n- EL-13.39: No overhead facilities on plan\n\nThese three findings all address overhead distribution requirements and should be merged into one comment.\n\n### Group G: Vertical Clearance & Cross-Sections (Findings 26, 31)\n- EL-13.26: No cross-section showing vertical relationships (transformer over/under utilities)\n- EL-13.31: No cross-section showing vertical clearances in access paths\n\nBoth findings require cross-section drawings to show vertical relationships and clearances.\n\n### Group H: Easement & Private Property Compliance (Findings 34, 35, 36,\n33)\n- EL-13.34: Transformer in vault easement, undepicted easements, no AE Design approval\n- EL-13.35: Transformer easements not shown on plan\n- EL-13.36: Electrical routing 100% on private property not confirmed\n- EL-13.33: Pads relative to recorded easements not clearly documented\n\nThese four findings all relate to easement conflicts and private property documentation requirements, so they should be consolidated.\n\n### Group I: Pad Design Specifications (Findings 37, 38)\n- EL-13.37: Pad dimensions not specified, no reference to AE Design pad detail sheets\n- EL-13.38: Bollard protection missing for pads near parking/vehicle routes\n\nPad dimensions are a specification issue while bollard protection is a safety measure—these address different concerns and should remain separate.\n\n### Group J: Fire Lane\n\n, Sidewalk, & Fence Clearances (Findings 3, 7, 19)\n- EL-13.3: Fence clearance (5 feet with operators, 3 feet without)\n- EL-13.7: Sidewalk clearance not dimensioned\n- EL-13.19: Fire lane clearance (15 feet)\n\nAll three findings involve clearance distances from various site features, making them good candidates for consolidation.\n\n### Group K: Utility-Specific (Fire Escape, Niche Service)\n- EL-13.9: Fire escape clearance (20 feet, oil-filled only)\n- EL-13.29: Niche service clearance (35 feet vertical inside niche)\n- EL-13.30: Niche service fire rating and architectural specs\n\nThe niche service findings (EL-13.29 and EL-13.30) are closely related, but the fire escape clearance stands apart as a distinct utility requirement.\n\nLooking more closely at the run distribution: EL-13.9 appears in runs 2 and 3 for oil-filled equipment, while EL-13.29 and EL-13.30 only appear in run 2 and specifically apply when Pad 3 is located within the vault easement. The triggering conditions differ—one depends on equipment type, the others on site configuration—so keeping EL-13.9 separate while merging the niche-specific pair makes more sense.\n\nFor gas line clearance, EL-13.24 stands alone since no gas service appears on the plan. Moving to the broader organization, I'm grouping findings into categories: transformer specifications, building and structure setbacks, access door positioning, utility line separations (water, sewer, storm, electric), vegetation buffers, site infrastructure spacing (fence, sidewalk, fire lane, dumpster, bike rack, light pole), overhead distribution lines, and vertical clearance with cross-section details.\n\nAdding easement and property line compliance, transformer pad sizing, bollard safeguards, and fire escape with niche service requirements brings the total to 13 distinct comment groups across 38 findings—roughly a 3:1 ratio. I can compress further by consolidating site feature spacing into one broader category and combining overhead line requirements with related clearance standards.\n\nLooking at the infrastructure clearances more carefully, sidewalk, fence, fire lane, dumpster, bike rack, and light pole all follow the same pattern of unspecified spacing ranges between 5 and 15 feet. These could roll into a single \"Site Feature Clearances\" comment rather than staying separate. For overhead distribution and vertical clearances, those address different concerns—one focuses on overhead lines specifically, the other on cross-sectional documentation needs—so keeping them distinct makes sense.\n\nThe transformer type specification and fire escape/niche requirements are actually linked: the fire escape and niche clearances only apply depending on whether the transformer is oil-filled or dry-type. Merging these as \"Transformer Specification & Type-Dependent Requirements\" would capture the transformer type selection along with the conditional clearance documentation that follows from it. 5 feet minimum) not dimensioned\n   - EL-13.5: No cross-section showing whether structures overhang pads\n   - Base issue: Clearance dimensions not verified\n\n3. **Access Door Orientation Not Documented**\n   - EL-13.10: Hot-stick clearance requires access door orientation (10 feet vs other sides)\n   - EL-13.11: Confined space clearance depends on access door orientation and vertical clearance\n   - EL-13.14: Shrub clearance (10 feet vs 5 feet) depends on access door location\n   - Base issue: Missing access door orientation prevents verification of multiple clearances\n\n4. **Utility Line Clearances Undimensioned**\n   - EL-13.21: Water line (5 feet)\n   - EL-13.22: Wastewater line (5 feet)\n   - EL-13.23: Storm drain (5 feet) — includes critical FAIL for\n\nThe structural clearances from building elements lack dimensioning on the plans. Access door placement is undocumented, which cascades through several clearance requirements—hot-stick access, confined space rules, and vegetation setbacks all hinge on knowing which side the door faces. Utility line separations also need verification against the 5-foot standard across water, wastewater, and storm drainage.\n\nLandscape elements require species identification and clearance specifications, while site features like fences, hydrants, dumpsters, and bike racks all need dimensioned setbacks. Overhead distribution lines and conductors aren't located on the electrical plan, and vertical relationships between the transformer and surrounding utilities lack cross-sectional documentation.\n\nEasement documentation needs clarification—the transformer pad location within the vault easement requires AE Design approval, easement boundaries aren't shown, and confirmation that electrical routing stays entirely on private property is missing. Additionally, the pad itself lacks specified dimensions and construction details, with the service type (single or three-phase) not identified.\n\nPad 1 positioned near the main driveway requires 4-inch bollards within a 4-foot radius of vehicle routes, but this protection isn't depicted on the plan. Fire escape clearance requirements and niche service specifications depend on confirming whether the equipment is oil-filled and whether Pad 3 functions as a niche service—both of which hinge on identifying the transformer type and service configuration. specified, and whether gas service is present—all of which affect clearance and design requirements. Building setbacks lack dimensioning for the required 5-foot minimum, and there's no cross-section confirming structures don't encroach above the pads. Access door placement determines hot-stick clearance (10 feet) and confined space requirements, while also affecting vegetation clearance thresholds (10 feet versus 5 feet). Underground utility separations remain undimensioned.\n\nWater, wastewater, storm, and electric lines all lack horizontal and vertical separation dimensions, with the storm drain flagged as a failure for Pad 3. The landscape plan doesn't specify tree species or their utility compatibility per ECM Appendix F, and shrub clearances aren't dimensioned. Root barriers for trees within 20 feet of the pad aren't shown. Site features—fences, sidewalks, fire hydrants, dumpsters, bike racks, light poles, and fire lanes—all lack documented clearances, which range from 3 to 15 feet depending on the feature and orientation. Overhead distribution lines aren't shown on the electrical plan, making the required 7.5-foot horizontal and 15-foot radial clearances unverifiable.\n\nCross-sections are missing to show vertical relationships between transformers and utilities, as well as vertical clearances in access paths. Transformer Pad 3 sits within a vault easement without AE Design approval, and recorded easements like the Greenbelt Access and City of Austin Drainage aren't depicted. Transformer easement widths and boundaries aren't shown, and whether electrical routing stays entirely on private property isn't confirmed. Pad dimensions, service type, and references to approved detail sheets are absent. Pad 1 is positioned within 4 feet of the main driveway, requiring vehicle impact protection that isn't documented. Comment 5 appears to be the right fit since the core issue involves missing clearance dimensions on the landscape plan itself. EL-13.14 addresses shrub placement relative to transformer access doors, but the primary deficiency is that the landscape documentation doesn't specify the required clearances—whether 10 feet or 5 feet depends on door orientation, which adds complexity to how this should be documented in the plan. covers the electrical plan deficiency. So EL-13.14 belongs in Comment 5 because the primary corrective action is updating the landscape plan with clearance dimensions from transformer pads, accounting for the 10-foot access door side versus 5-foot other sides requirement. The access door orientation dependency is already addressed in Comment 3.\n\nFor EL-13.4, I need to incorporate this finding about reduced clearances. The core issue is that without clearance dimensions on the plans, I can't verify whether any transformer pads qualify for the reduced 3-foot clearance option, which would require Austin Energy Design written approval and documentation of four specific conditions: location outside confined spaces, minimum 10 feet from windows/doors/ducts, 15 feet vertical clearance, and 3-hour fire rating. Pad 3 appears to be positioned approximately 4-6 feet from a structure, which would fall short of the required clearances for standard installation. dimensions aren't shown on the plans, so we can't verify whether a reduced clearance is actually being used. I should consolidate EL-13.4 into Comment 2, noting that any reduced clearances would require AE Design approval documentation.\n\nEL-13.8 addresses fire escapes and stairwells not appearing on the electrical or fire protection plans near transformer locations. This differs from EL-13.9, which specifies the 20-foot clearance requirement for oil-filled equipment. Since EL-13.8 only appears in one run comment, it's a lower-confidence item focused on documentation gaps rather than a specific dimensional requirement. The core issue is that fire escapes aren't documented on the plans, making it impossible to verify the 20-foot clearance requirement for oil-filled equipment. EL-13.8 addresses the missing documentation while EL-13.9 addresses the clearance requirement itself—they're related but separate findings.\n\nSince the 20-foot distance is conditional on equipment type (oil-filled), this belongs in the type-dependent requirements section. The fire escape documentation gap should be noted as supporting detail for why the clearance can't be verified. I'm mixing too many specifications together. Let me separate by what each specification actually controls. Equipment type—whether oil-filled or dry-type—determines window/door clearances, drainage requirements, and fire escape setbacks. Service configuration (single vs. three-phase) drives pad sizing. Niche vs. standard installation and gas service presence each have their own verification requirements. I should group findings by these controlling factors rather than trying to consolidate everything into broad categories. I'm consolidating these into a single comment since they all stem from missing equipment and service specifications. The key issue is that multiple code requirements hinge on decisions not yet documented—whether the transformer is oil-filled or dry-type, whether gas service is included, and whether Pad 3 uses standard or niche configuration. Each of these unknowns cascades into different clearance, drainage, and fire-rating requirements. Rather than flagging each dependency separately, one comprehensive comment listing all affected code sections makes the interdependencies clearer and gives the design team a complete picture of what needs to be determined upfront. Landscape specifications need species identification and clearance dimensions, particularly for plantings near the access door—which depends on resolving the door orientation issue from the previous group. Site features including hardscape elements, drainage, and outdoor structures lack documented clearances to utilities and property lines. Overhead distribution lines and their clearance zones remain unlocated on the plans, creating uncertainty about conflicts with building features and landscape elements.\n\nCross-sectional views showing vertical clearances for the building envelope and site features are missing entirely. Easement boundaries and private property lines need verification against the proposed improvements to ensure compliance with recorded restrictions. The transformer pad requires vehicle impact protection design. Overall, the consolidation captures 38 unique findings across 10 distinct issues, each requiring separate corrective actions.\n\nNow I'm organizing the findings into comment groups and starting to draft the actual review comments. The first comment addresses transformer equipment and service specifications that are absent from the electrical plans—covering nameplate data, protection coordination, grounding details, and maintenance access requirements. The transformer specifications lack critical details needed to verify clearance and design requirements. Without confirmation of equipment type (oil-filled versus dry-type), I cannot validate window/door/duct clearances of 12 feet for oil-filled units or 10 feet where openings exist within that distance. Drainage requirements away from buildings apply only to oil-filled transformers, making verification impossible without this specification. Fire escape clearances of 20 feet are contingent on oil-filled equipment, yet fire escapes aren't identified on the plans. Gas utility service presence remains undocumented, which affects the 24-inch horizontal clearance requirement if such service is proposed.\n\nFor Transformer Pad 3, if it's configured as a niche service installation per the Vault Easement notation, the 35-foot minimum vertical clearance inside the niche and at its entrance cannot be confirmed. Should this be a niche service, the 3-hour fire rating for walls and ceilings, ventilation specifications, and Austin Energy Design business unit consultation documentation are all missing. Additionally, the transformer pad dimensions and service configuration (single-phase or three-phase) aren't specified on the electrical plan, preventing reference to AE Design-approved detail sheets.\n\nClearances between transformer pads and adjacent building structures lack dimensioning, and cross-sectional details showing vertical relationships and overhead structures are missing. If reduced 3-foot clearances are being applied instead of the standard 5-foot minimum, Austin Energy Design written approval with supporting documentation would be required but hasn't been provided. The access door orientation for each transformer pad isn't documented on the electrical plan. This matters because clearance requirements shift based on which direction the door faces—hot-stick operations need 10 feet of clearance on the access side, while vegetation and obstructions have different distance requirements depending on door location. All three runs consistently identified this gap across multiple findings, indicating medium severity. The electrical plan needs directional arrows showing access door orientation for every transformer pad. transformer pads not documented on electrical plan; 10-foot clearance (access door side) and 5-foot clearance (other sides) requirements cannot be verified without access door orientation and landscape plan dimensions.\n\nUnderground utility lines including water, wastewater, storm drain, and electric are positioned near transformer pads but lack horizontal and vertical separation dimensions. The required 5-foot minimum clearance from water, wastewater, and storm drain utilities and 12-inch minimum from underground electric cannot be confirmed. Storm drain proximity to Transformer Pad 3 presents particular concern based on sight line analysis. clearance per UCM § 2.9.1.B.2 cannot be verified without marked dimensions. Wastewater lines near transformer pads (TP3 shows WW line roughly 5-10 feet west) also lack labeled clearance dimensions. The storm drain presents the most critical issue—it runs through the corridor between Buildings 4 and 5 where Pad 3 sits, with vision analysis suggesting the line passes directly beneath or extremely close to the pad with minimal or no separation. Profile documentation is essential to confirm adequate clearance.\n\nMultiple underground utilities clustered near transformer pads lack horizontal and vertical separation dimensions. The 12-inch minimum clearance requirements from other utilities cannot be verified without marked dimensions or detailed utility profiles. Immediate clarification is needed, potentially requiring pad relocation depending on what the survey reveals.\n\nThe landscape plan identifies trees and shrubs by category codes only, without specifying actual species or documenting clearance requirements from utilities. Root barrier specifications must be incorporated into the construction documents. The landscape plan shows trees along property perimeters with a species list, but lacks utility-compatibility verification against ECM Appendix F and doesn't depict transformer pad locations, making it impossible to confirm the required 10-foot and 15-foot lateral clearances. Similarly, shrub and vegetation placement dimensions relative to transformer pads and access doors are unmarked, with no indication of the required 10-foot clearance from access door sides and 5-foot clearance from other sides.\n\nFor trees within 20 lateral feet of transformer pads, root barrier details specifying 4-foot minimum depth and 5-foot distance from equipment per UCM § 1.10.10.4 are not shown on either the landscape plan or construction detail sheets, despite trees being present near all transformer pads. Additionally, access door orientation must be documented on the electrical plan to enable proper clearance verification.\n\nSeven site features—fence, sidewalk, fire hydrant, dumpster, bike rack, light pole, and fire lane—lack documented clearances in the construction documents. Fences are depicted on the site plan (pond perimeter, wood fence along the east boundary, pond access gates), but their exact positions relative to transformer pads and clearance distances aren't dimensioned. Per UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4, removable ventilated fences need 5-foot clearance from pad sides with operator controls and 3-foot clearance from sides without—verification requires knowing access door orientation and marked dimensions.\n\nSidewalks appear on Sheets 9 and 21, with several transformer pads positioned adjacent to sidewalk areas (TP1 and TP2 on the western side notably), yet no clearance dimensions from the back of sidewalk to pad edges are labeled per UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4.\n\nFire hydrants are marked on Sheet 10 (two proposed locations), but clearance dimensions to the five transformer pads on Sheet 21 aren't provided. The 5-foot minimum clearance per Reviewer Convention can't be confirmed without marked dimensions, especially given one hydrant's proximity to the transformer pad southeast of Building 7. Dumpster and trash enclosure locations aren't shown on the site plan adjacent to transformer pads, and if present, clearance dimensions aren't documented—the 5-foot minimum per Reviewer Convention can't be evaluated without identifying dumpster locations. Bike parking (41 spaces per Sheet 9) is shown elsewhere on site, but its specific proximity to transformer pads lacks clearance dimensions, preventing verification of the 5-foot minimum per Reviewer Convention.\n\nLight pole locations adjacent to transformer pads aren't marked on the site plan with clearance dimensions per Reviewer Convention, so the 5-foot minimum clearance can't be verified. Fire lanes loop through the site with 26-foot width per Sheet 10; Transformer Pad 1 at the northwest sits adjacent to the main entrance driveway serving as a fire lane, with visual analysis suggesting it's only 2-5 feet from the fire lane edge—potentially violating the 15-foot minimum clearance per Reviewer Convention. No explicit dimension labels the clearance on the plans.\n\nOverhead distribution line routes and clearance dimensions from transformer pads aren't documented on electrical or utility plans, making it impossible to verify compliance with 7.5-foot horizontal sky-to-ground clearance and 15-foot radius clearance requirements. The electrical plan needs to identify and locate overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors with clearance dimensions from transformer pad locations, and if overhead lines exist on adjacent properties, the AE Design should confirm clearance requirements. 3 cannot be verified. Cross-sectional drawings showing vertical relationships between transformer pads and underground utilities are absent, as are profiles documenting clearances in access paths relative to overhead structures. UCM § 1.10.3 and § 1.10.5.B prohibit transformers and AE facilities from being positioned directly over or under other utility lines, and customer buildings cannot be placed over AE underground facilities. Without sectional documentation, I cannot confirm vertical compliance.\n\nThe utility profile and cross-section sheets need to demonstrate vertical relationships between transformer pads and all nearby underground utilities—water, sewer, storm drain, electric—along with vertical clearance in access paths showing the minimum 15-foot requirement where applicable. The Grading Plan indicates varying topographic grades, but cross-sections are necessary to clarify these vertical relationships. EL-13.26 lacks any profile or cross-section drawing showing how transformer pads relate vertically to utility lines running beneath or above them.\n\nWithout these profile views, I can't verify compliance with the prohibition against positioning transformers or AE facilities over/under other utility lines, or placing customer buildings over AE underground facilities. Multiple underground utilities are shown near the transformer pads, but their vertical separation remains undocumented. For EL-13.31, cross-section drawings are missing entirely to show vertical clearances in access paths with overhead structures, parking garages, or enclosed areas—Runs 1 and 3 are flagged as failures. The topographic contours on Sheet 11 suggest grade changes that could affect clearance to transformer access areas, making these cross-sections critical for compliance verification.\n\nFour recorded easements (Greenbelt Access & Use, City of Austin Drainage, 25' Drainage Easement, Public Utility/Electric Easements) aren't clearly marked on the electrical plan. Transformer Pad 3 sits within a Vault Easement, and placing equipment in prohibited easements requires Austin Energy Design written approval—which hasn't been provided. The easement boundaries and widths aren't depicted, and I can't confirm that electrical routing from transformers to meters stays entirely on private property.\n\nThe title commitment needs to identify all recorded easements, and the electrical plan should explicitly show which transformer pads fall within or outside easement areas, along with any required Austin Energy Design approvals. Most critically, I need to verify Pad 3's position relative to the Vault Easement and obtain written approval if equipment placement within that easement is necessary.\n\nTwo significant easements are recorded on title but missing from the site plan: a Greenbelt Access & Use Easement (0.31 acres in the NE interior as a no-build zone) and a City of Austin Drainage Easement (0.130 acres at the NE corner). Without these depicted, I cannot confirm transformer pad locations against them. The site plan's note about \"additional electric easements may be required\" signals incomplete documentation.\n\nFor the transformer easements themselves, the electrical sheets lack the required rectangular dashed boundary lines and dimension labels showing the easement widths that Austin Energy Design must determine for accessibility. Without these marked boundaries, I cannot verify compliance with the easement width requirements.\n\nElectrical routing from transformers to meters appears on Sheet 21 but lacks explicit notation confirming the entire path stays on private property. Meter locations relative to recorded easements and property lines need clarification to verify this routing requirement. Additionally, transformer pad placement must be 100% on private property, yet Sheet 2 shows multiple overlapping easements—including 15' and 5' public utility easements, a 5' electric easement, a 25' drainage easement, and a 10' water easement—making it unclear whether the pads avoid all of them.\n\nTransformer Pad 1 presents another issue: it sits in the northwest corner near the main entrance driveway within the vehicle circulation zone but lacks the required 4-inch galvanized rigid metal bollard protection. Other pads on site have bollards installed, so this omission at Pad 1 is inconsistent and fails to meet the equipment pad protection standard. Pad 1 lacks bollard protection despite being within the 4-foot vehicle proximity threshold at the main entrance. While bollards are detailed at other transformer pads on the east side, Pad 1's critical location requires clarification on actual setback distances and confirmation of whether bollard installation is needed per code requirements.\n\nNow structuring the JSON output with required fields: headline (3-12 words), summary (~30 words max 50), sheets array, applicableArea, severity (0-3), confidence (1-3), crossDep, attn, and details array. Each detail object includes text, citation, sourceId, confidence based on run frequency, and runComments tracking findings across runs. Confidence levels derive from run counts—three runs yields confidence 3, two runs yields confidence 2, single run yields confidence 1.\n\nFor Comment 1, the mix of findings across runs (EL-13.1 and EL-13.4 appearing twice, EL-13.5 once) gives overall confidence 2. Comment 2 similarly shows confidence 2 with EL-13.1 and EL-13.4 at two runs each, EL-13.5 at one. Comment 3 reaches confidence 3 since all three findings (EL-13.10, EL-13.11, EL-13.14) appear across multiple runs, with EL-13.10 and EL-13.14 hitting all three runs. Comment 4 starts with EL-13.21, EL-13.22, and EL-13.23 all appearing across three runs.\n\nI notice EL-13.14 appears in both Comment 3 and Comment 5, so I need to determine its proper placement. The finding involves two separate deficiencies—access door orientation missing from the electrical plan and shrub clearances not dimensioned on the landscape plan. Since these stem from different plan documents, I should evaluate whether it belongs with the electrical findings or the landscape findings based on the root cause.\n\nThe shrub clearance dimension actually depends on the access door orientation being established first, making the landscape plan deficiency conditional on resolving the electrical plan issue. This suggests EL-13.14 fits better in Comment 5 (Landscape Plan) as the primary categorization, with the understanding that Comment 3 (access door orientation) must be addressed beforehand. Comment 5 then includes EL-13.13, EL-13.14, and EL-13.15, all flagged across the three runs, maintaining confidence level 3.\n\nMoving through the remaining findings: EL-13.3, EL-13.7, EL-13.12, EL-13.16, EL-13.17, EL-13.18, and EL-13.19 cluster into Comment 6 with confidence 2. Comment 7 groups EL-13.27, EL-13.28, and EL-13.39 at confidence 2. Comment 8 contains EL-13.26 and EL-13.31 (both appearing across all runs), along with EL-13.34, EL-13.35, EL-13.36, and EL-13.33, establishing confidence 3 for this grouping.\n\nEL-13.38 stands alone in Comment 10 with three runs supporting confidence 3. Now I need to structure this as JSON while keeping per-run comments intact and trimming detail text to the 30-50 word target, removing redundant framing language and focusing on specific data points.\n\nChecking citations against the source findings—most reference UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4 or its footnotes, with EL-13.8 citing Reviewer Convention and EL-13.24 citing UCM § 1.10.5.A. The detail texts need tightening without sacrificing critical information.","signature":""}],"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"06296f4d-2654-4f82-83f2-dd087149eb49","uuid":"efb8b8aa-0084-4b8b-a6e2-7e0e0b892ccf"}
{"level":30,"time":1778246903299,"pid":122,"hostname":"bd047d16-36d","step":"synthesize-comments","item":"13.json","index":2,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen_01KR3W7YXT697F6K72KE0CZCH6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Looking at this grouping, I'm reviewing **38 consolidated transformer infrastructure findings** across **3 runs** of review. The findings cluster around electrical utility (transformer) placement, clearances, easements, and associated infrastructure conflicts.\n\n## Scope Assessment\n\n**Discipline:** Utility (Electrical / Transformer Infrastructure)\n\nAll findings are **Tier 1 (Within Scope)**. The electrical utility discipline owns transformer placement compliance. A few findings involve cross-departmental coordination (landscaping, grading, structural, stormwater) but remain utility-primary issues.\n\n**Governing Standard:** UCM § 1.10.x (Utility Code Manual, Transformer Placement & Clearance Requirements), with references to ECM Appendix F (utility-compatible trees) and Reviewer Convention (standard practices).\n\n## Consolidation Strategy\n\n38 findings merge into **10 actionable comments** (~3.8:1 ratio). Merging is aggressive where findings share:\n- Same regulatory section (e.g., UCM § 1.10.4 clearance rules)\n- Same infrastructure element (e.g., water/wastewater/storm drain clearances)\n- Same corrective action (e.g., \"add access door orientation to electrical plan\")\n- Same underlying deficiency type (e.g., \"clearance dimension missing\")\n\nKept separate where different actions are required (e.g., Pad dimensions ≠ Bollard protection).\n\n---"}],"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"06296f4d-2654-4f82-83f2-dd087149eb49","uuid":"58b31540-b6fc-4c5f-8687-a45f7a43f416"}
{"level":30,"time":1778246939097,"pid":122,"hostname":"bd047d16-36d","step":"synthesize-comments","item":"1.json","index":3,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen_01KR3W7YYK9G292C2WYKQVEG5W","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze this review comment synthesis task carefully. I'm processing consolidated findings for what appears to be an electrical utility compliance review (based on the finding IDs like EL-1.1, EL-1.2, etc., which reference electrical utility requirements).\n\n## STEP 1: Discipline Scope\n\nFrom the finding IDs and content:\n- All findings relate to overhead electric lines (OHE)\n- References to UCM § 1.10.3 (clearance requirements)\n- References to UCM § 1.10.6.2 (driveway clearance)\n- References to utility infrastructure, poles, conductors, transformers\n- All relate to electrical infrastructure clearances and placement\n\n**Discipline:** Utility (Electrical) or Infrastructure/Utility Coordination\n\n**Primary Concern:** Clearance requirements from overhead electric lines and poles to buildings, driveways, structures, trees, equipment, and other site features; also electrical infrastructure placement and easement compliance.\n\nThis is clearly within the **Utility** discipline scope, specifically electrical utility compliance. All findings are Tier 1 — they directly impact electrical utility requirements.\n\n## STEP 2: Program & Hierarchy\n\nLooking at the citations:\n- UCM § 1.10.3 (primary citation appearing repeatedly) - this is likely a municipal code section\n- UCM § 1.10.6.2 - vertical clearance requirements\n- ECM § 2.4.1.D, ECM § 2.4.2.C - tree species requirements\n- Texas Health & Safety Code Chapter 752, Section 752.003 - notification requirements\n- Reviewer Convention - standard practices\n\nThe governing standards appear to be:\n1. UCM (likely city/utility municipal code) - primary electrical utility requirements\n2. ECM (likely environmental or landscaping code) - tree compatibility\n3. Texas state law - notification requirements\n\nAll citations appear legitimate and appropriately applied by the review agents.\n\n## STEP 3: Consolidation Strategy\n\nLet me count the input findings:\n\n\n1. EL-1.1 - Building clearance from OHE (3 runs)\n2. EL-1.2 - Transformer pad clearance and conductor identification (3 runs)\n3. EL-1.3 - Building projections clearance (3 runs)\n4. EL-1.5 - Elevated walkways clearance (2 runs)\n5. EL-1.6 - Building setbacks and OHE clearance (3 runs)\n6. EL-1.7 - Missing building elevation sheets (3 runs)\n7. EL-1\n\n.8 - Missing section/plan dimension exhibits (3 runs)\n8. EL-1.9 - Fence clearance from conductors (3 runs)\n9. EL-1.10 - Fence posts to electric poles distance (3 runs)\n10. EL-1.12 - Transmission line fencing (1 run)\n11. EL-1.13 - Vehicle barrier fencing (1 run)\n12. EL-1.14 - Retaining wall clearance (3 runs)\n13. EL-1.16 - Emergency access structures (2 runs)\n14. EL-1.17 - Retaining wall section views (3 runs)\n15. EL-1.18 - Driveway vertical clearance (3 runs)\n16. EL-1.19 - Parking vertical clearance (2 runs)\n17. EL-1.20 - Transmission line vertical clearance (1 run)\n18. EL-1.22 - Utility pole location notation (2 runs)\n19. EL-1.23 - Clearance dimension methodology (3 runs)\n20. EL-1.24 - Streetlight pole location (2 runs)\n21. EL-1.25 - Sidewalk clearance from power poles (3 runs)\n22. EL-1.26 - Trash enclosure clearance (3 runs)\n23. EL-1.27 - Facilities within electric easements (3 runs)\n24. EL-1.28 - Water meter clearance from service drops (3 runs)\n25. EL-1.29 - Water line clearance from poles (3 runs)\n26. EL-1.30 - Building setbacks for future utility work (3 runs)\n27. EL-1.31 - Mid-span clearance survey (3 runs)\n28. EL-1.32 - OHE line crossing angle (2 runs)\n29. EL-1.33 - Secondary/primary line crossing (2 runs)\n30. EL-1.35 - Proposed power poles spacing (1 run)\n31. EL-1.36 - Decorative lighting clearance (3 runs)\n32. EL-1.37 - Tree species and utility compatibility (3 runs)\n33. EL-1.38 - Meter clear ance and working space (3 runs)\n34. EL-1.39 - Buildings adjacent to streetlight poles (2 runs)\n35. EL-1.40 - Down guy wire conflicts (3 runs)\n36. EL-1.42 - Down guy anchor easements (3 runs)\n37. EL-1.43 - Transformer vertical clearance (2 runs)\n38. EL-1.45 - Construction staging clearance (3 runs)\n39. EL-1.46 - Austin Energy notification requirement (2 runs)\n40. EL-1.47 - Service mast elevation details (2 runs)\n\n**Total: 40 input findings**\n\nTarget output: ~10 comments (roughly 1 per 4 findings)\n\n## Merge Strategy\n\nI'm identifying natural groupings to consolidate these findings. Group A covers building and structure clearances—items like horizontal setbacks from overhead equipment (7.5' minimum), projections such as overhangs and balconies, elevated walkways, emergency access structures, and parking clearance requirements. These all relate to maintaining safe distances between buildings/structures and OHE, so I can combine them into 1-2 consolidated comments. Group B addresses documentation gaps, including missing building elevation sheets, section/plan dimension exhibits, and ret\n\naining wall section views missing dimensions, plus clearance dimensions not shown and missing mid-span clearance survey data. These all point to incomplete drawings and dimensioning, so they merge into a single documentation comment. Group C focuses on perimeter features—retaining walls, fences, fence posts, transmission line fencing, and vehicle barriers—which can consolidate into one comment about boundary element clearances. Group D involves driveway and roadway concerns, including vertical clearance at driveways, transmission line clearance at roadways, utility pole notation, OHE line crossing angles, and secondary/primary line crossing issues.\n\nThese are specific clearance and crossing issues that should merge into one comment. Group E covers customer equipment—transformer pads, meters, service masts, and water meters—all requiring clearance and working space documentation, so they consolidate into a single comment about equipment clearances. Group F addresses utility infrastructure access through facilities within easements, building setbacks, proposed pole spacing, and guy anchor easements, which can become one comment on easement and access requirements. Group G includes tree species compatibility and decorative lighting clearance near overhead lines, potentially forming one or two comments depending on scope. Group H contains minor site services like sidewalk clearance from poles, trash enclosure clearance, and water line clearance, which could be addressed individually or grouped depending on priority.\n\nThe next items—buildings adjacent to streetlight poles, down guy wire conflicts, Austin Energy notification requirements, and construction staging clearance—represent a mix of site services and contractor requirements. The notification requirement and construction staging clearance are distinct issues; notification is a procedural requirement while staging clearance is a physical constraint, so they may warrant separate treatment or could be consolidated with other construction-phase notes depending on how the comments are structured overall.\n\nConsolidating findings into approximately 10 comments requires grouping by core technical issues rather than individual items. Missing building elevation documentation with surveyed conductor locations and clearance dimensions should anchor one comment, combining several findings about documentation gaps. Buildings and structures positioned too close to overhead lines or lacking clearance verification forms another logical grouping. Driveway and roadway crossings of overhead equipment without documented clearance specifications can be merged. Retaining walls, fences, and barriers near overhead lines without clearance documentation represent a distinct category. Electrical equipment—transformers, meters, service masts—positioned without verified clearances should be consolidated. Utility easement compliance and access requirements need to be addressed as well.\n\nSite accessory features like sidewalks, water lines, and trash areas positioned without clearance documentation warrant their own comment. Trees and decorative lighting near overhead lines lacking species verification or clearance documentation can be grouped together. The methodology for measuring clearances—specifically ensuring dimensions originate from the outside conductor as required—should be highlighted separately. Finally, contractor notification and construction-phase clearance documentation requirements should be consolidated into a single comment addressing missing procedural controls.\n\nThe documentation gaps are particularly significant. Missing building elevation sheets, section/plan dimension exhibits, and mid-span clearance survey data across all three runs represent a critical verification problem. Additionally, buildings 6, 7, and 8 show insufficient clearance from overhead electric lines where the 7.5' horizontal sky-to-ground clearance hasn't been verified and building setbacks lack proper OHE clearance documentation.\n\nBuilding projections including overhangs, awnings, and balconies lack clearance documentation, as do elevated walkways, emergency access structures, and parking areas—none have elevation views showing their relationship to conductors. For roadway crossings, the driveway vertical clearance (16-18 feet), transmission line vertical clearance (30 feet) at the roadway, utility pole relocation notes at driveway crossings, and OHE crossing angle all need documentation.\n\nRetaining walls along the southern boundary and perimeter fencing throughout the site are positioned near overhead lines without clearance dimensions or section views. Fence posts require verification of the 10-foot minimum distance to electric poles, and transmission line fencing specifications and vehicle barrier clearance need confirmation. Electrical equipment like transformer pads and conductor types also lack documented clearances.\n\nThree transformer pads are shown but lack detailed working clearance documentation—the 30\" width, 36\" depth, and 6.5' headroom requirements aren't verified. Service masts need confirmation of 3' clearance above roof and 18\" clearance within 4' of roof edges. Water meters mounted near electric service drops and meters attached to buildings require obstruction analysis. Buildings and features within electric easements don't have documented clearances, and building setbacks from property lines may be inadequate for utility work access. Down guy anchor easements aren't shown with their required 10' width and 5' extension past anchor points. Proposed power poles need to maintain 20-foot minimum spacing, and pole relocation plans are unclear.\n\nSite improvements throughout lack clearance verification—sidewalks near existing poles, trash enclosures near overhead lines, water lines near utility poles, and streetlight poles near buildings all need documented distances. Down guy wires at the northwest pole create potential conflicts with accessible paths and driveways. Trees positioned near overhead lines require species verification for utility compatibility.\n\nThe fundamental issue is that clearance dimensions aren't shown on the plans at all—there's no way to verify whether measurements originate from the conductor or pole center. This same gap affects conductor type identification, since primary, secondary, and neutral lines aren't labeled, making the 15-foot radial clearance requirement impossible to confirm.\n\nFor the contractor phase, the plans omit the required 48-hour notification to Austin Energy and don't document construction staging areas or their clearance zones relative to the overhead lines.\n\nConsolidating items 10 and 11 brings the total to 11 comments covering 40 findings—roughly a 3.6:1 ratio. I could push toward 4:1 by merging the building-related comments further. Combining the insufficient clearance issues with building projections and elevated structures into a single consolidated comment on all building-related clearances would reduce the count, though it would create a longer, more detailed item that encompasses multiple clearance scenarios.\n\nIf I merge those two, the count drops to 10 comments, which feels more balanced. Comments 5 and 8 should stay separate since electrical equipment differs meaningfully from site services. Comments 6 and 8 have some overlap—one addresses easement compliance and utility access space, while the other covers site services and accessory features—but they're distinct enough to warrant keeping them apart.\n\nLooking at the structure, I'm settling on 10 comments total. The first addresses missing elevation sheets and clearance verification documents across three items. The second consolidates building and structure clearance issues from six separate findings, covering insufficient horizontal and vertical clearance documentation for buildings, projections, elevated walkways, emergency structures, and parking areas.\n\nThe remaining categories address driveways and roadways crossing overhead lines—five items involving vertical clearance and pole placement specifications; retaining walls and perimeter fences positioned near overhead equipment without clearance documentation across six findings; electrical equipment clearances and working space for transformers, meters, and service connections lacking verification across five items; and utility easement compliance issues including features within easements and inadequate access provisions across four findings.\n\nSite improvements like sidewalks, water lines, trash enclosures, and streetlights require clearance documentation that's currently missing. Landscape features present a separate concern—tree species haven't been verified against utility compatibility standards, and lighting clearance remains undocumented. Clearance documentation itself needs standardization, particularly around measurement methodology and conductor identification, since dimensions are either absent or not measured from the outside conductor boundary.\n\nThe final category addresses contractor and construction-phase requirements, specifically the need for Austin Energy notification and documented staging clearances during construction.\n\nLooking at EL-1.2 more closely, it spans two distinct issues: transformer pad clearance dimensions and conductor type identification. Since the primary concern across all three runs centers on verifying the 15-foot radial clearance requirement for transformers relative to overhead lines, this belongs in the electrical equipment category. The conductor classification aspect is secondary to the main clearance verification problem, so EL-1.2 stays grouped with transformer-related items, leaving Comment 9 to focus solely on measurement and dimensional documentation like EL-1.23. 39, EL-1.40\n\n8. Landscape Features - Tree Species and Clearances\n   - EL-1.37, EL-1.36\n\n9. Clearance Documentation Standards and Measurement Methodology\n   - EL-1.23\n\n10. Contractor and Construction-Phase Requirements\n    - EL-1.46, EL-1.45\n\nThe count comes to 39 items across all categories, but I need to verify against the complete input list. Going through the findings systematically: EL-1.1 through EL-1.47 with some gaps. Let me cross-check each code against what's been assigned to ensure nothing's been duplicated or missed. I'm checking my categorization of the items. Looking at EL-1.24 about streetlight poles and their relationship to overhead line locations—this concerns site improvement clearances rather than measurement methodology, so it belongs with the site improvements comment. That means Comment 7 should include EL-1.25, EL-1.26, EL-1.29, EL-1.39, EL-1.40, and EL-1.24, bringing it to six items. The pole location documentation and building-to-pole clearance issues are distinct but can coexist in the same grouping. My total now reaches 40 items across all comments, which matches the expected count.\n\nHowever, I should reconsider the naming of Comment 7 to better reflect its scope. \"Site Improvements and Infrastructure - Clearance Documentation\" might be more accurate, though EL-1.39 specifically addresses building-to-pole clearance rather than general equipment clearance. This raises a question about whether it fits better in the buildings and structures category, but that comment focuses on overhead line clearances, not pole clearances, so the current placement seems more appropriate.\n\nThe core issue across these items—EL-1.25, EL-1.26, EL-1.29, EL-1.39, EL-1.40, and EL-1.24—is that site accessories and existing utility infrastructure lack documented clearance verification. Now I'll structure these findings in JSON format.\n\nEach comment requires specific fields: a concise headline, brief summary, affected sheets, applicable zone, severity and confidence ratings, cross-departmental notes, and an array of detailed findings with citations and run history. I'm starting with the building elevation and clearance documentation gaps.\n\nFor the first finding, the core issue is that elevation sheets, section/plan dimensions, and surveyor clearance data are absent, making it impossible to verify overhead electric facility clearances. This is a critical gap since the 7.5-foot and 15-foot clearance requirements can't be validated without these documents. The three code sections (EL-1.7, EL-1.8, EL-1.31) all flagged this consistently across runs, indicating high confidence. Sheets 9 and 21 are the relevant plan documents. Since this is fundamentally a documentation deficiency rather than a spatial issue, \"Plan Documentation\" is the appropriate area. No cross-departmental coordination is needed for this particular finding. Required building elevation sheets showing conductor heights and clearances to building features are missing. The site plan package