an view shows buildings set back from property lines, but surveyed elevation sheets with conductor heights and clearance dimensions are not provided to confirm compliance with 7.5-foot horizontal and 15-foot radial clearance requirements.\"}\n  - {runNum: 2, status: \"fail\", comment: \"No elevation documentation demonstrates that buildings maintain required clearances from overhead conductors. Setbacks shown in plan are insufficient without vertical clearance verification.\"}\n  - {runNum: 3, status: \"nv\", comment: \"Sheet 9 shows building positions relative to overhead lines, but lacks surveyed elevation views with dimensioned clearance measurements from the outside conductor to verify both horizontal and radial separation requirements.\"}\n\nThe core issue across both details centers on the absence of surveyed building elevations with dimensioned clearance measurements from overhead conductors. Plan views alone cannot establish compliance with the 7.5-foot horizontal sky-to-ground clearance or the 15-foot radial clearance requirements. Each review run consistently identifies this gap—whether noting the information as not verified or failing the requirement outright. Elevation sheets showing conductor positions and distances to building facades are essential to demonstrate code compliance. or sections showing surveyed conductor elevations and clearances from building projections to overhead conductors. Building elevation sheets with conductor locations are needed to verify the required clearances from overhangs, awnings, balconies, decks, roofs, and patios.\n\nElevated walkways and pedestrian platforms appear on the site plan without documented clearance dimensions from the OHE lines. Elevation or section drawings are missing to confirm the required 15-foot radial clearance is maintained.\n\nEmergency access structures like elevated platforms or bridges aren't clearly marked on the site plan, and if they exist, the necessary section views demonstrating required clearances from overhead conductors haven't been submitted.\n\nDetail 6 - EL-1.19 identifies that while vehicle parking areas appear on the site plan, there's no explicit documentation of vehicle display pads or vertical clearance measurements for parking zones near overhead electric lines, per UCM § 1.10.6.2. This detail also shows confidence level 2 with runs 2 and 3 flagged. Driveways crossing overhead electric lines lack documented vertical clearance verification, utility pole coordination, or crossing angle specifications. The required 16-18 foot clearance for parking-area driveways and 30-foot clearance for transmission lines are unverified across sheets 9, 11, and 4. This represents a critical safety and compliance issue flagged consistently across all three review runs.\n\nThe western driveway entrance crosses beneath the OHE line at multiple points, requiring confirmation of whether existing poles will be relocated, converted to self-supporting structures, or if the driveway alignment needs adjustment. Austin Energy input is needed for crossing specifications, and utility coordination is essential for any pole relocation work.\n\nFinished grade spot elevations ranging from 679.75 to 688.40 feet are proposed at crossing locations, but no vertical clearance dimensions verify the required minimums, and mid-span clearance survey data has not been provided. The driveway entrances on the western boundary cross beneath the existing overhead electric line, with finished grades ranging from 679.75 to 688.40 feet shown on the grading plan. However, no vertical clearance dimensions are provided to verify compliance with the 16-foot minimum (for parking without truck traffic) or 18-foot minimum (for truck traffic areas), and surveyed mid-span conductor elevation data is absent. The grading plan also lacks the required 30-foot vertical clearance dimensions from transmission lines to final grade elevations for driveways and roads within the transmission easement.\n\nAddi